msgid "Snapmaker J1"
msgstr ""

#. More: intro
msgctxt "idex-alignment/download"
msgid ""
"One file per **test** (Lite or Vernier) and per **bed size**. PrusaSlicer "
"centres the test on your bed: the preset only decides how far out the "
"stations go, about 10 mm from each edge."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/download"
msgid ""
"Beds of 300 mm and more get **9 stations** (corners, edge midpoints and "
"centre); smaller beds get **5** (corners and centre)."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/download"
msgid ""
"Download the **single .3mf file**, not \"Download all\" as a ZIP: "
"PrusaSlicer opens a ZIP's 3MF as plain geometry and the tool assignment is "
"lost."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/download"
msgid ""
"Filament and time, PETG at a 0.2 mm first layer: **Lite** 0.17 g (5 "
"stations) to 0.32 g (9 stations), a few minutes. **Vernier** 0.66 g to 1.19 "
"g, about 3.5 to 17 minutes depending on your first-layer speed. A 0.3 mm "
"first layer uses about 50 % more. These are our estimates; PrusaSlicer "
"shows the real figures after slicing."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "idex-alignment/download"
msgid ""
"**Other beds, nozzles or tool pairs:** one command in the {suite} "
"[repository](link:repository), with Python 3.11 and nothing else. `python3 "
"-I calib.py idex-vernier --bed 330x330` for a custom bed, `--nozzle 0.6` "
"for another nozzle (files end in `_n0.6`), `--ref-tool 1 --tool 3` to "
"compare other tools of a toolchanger (files end in `_T1-T3`). Read such "
"files with `calib.py idex-analyze` and the same options: the card only "
"knows the presets."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "idex-alignment/prepare"
msgid "Prepare the printer and the slicer"
msgstr ""

#. callout title
msgctxt "idex-alignment/prepare"
msgid "Tool offsets in one place only"
msgstr ""

#. callout
msgctxt "idex-alignment/prepare"
msgid ""
"The XY offset between the tools can live in the **firmware** or in the "
"**slicer**. Use one, and set the other to zero. Corrected in both places, "
"every change counts twice."
msgstr ""

#. callout item
msgctxt "idex-alignment/prepare"
msgid ""
"**RatOS** keeps the offsets in the firmware (`idex_xoffset`, "
"`idex_yoffset`, set by VAOC or by hand). Then the slicer's **Extruder "
"offset** must be **0, 0** for every extruder (Printer Settings › Extruder 2 "
"› Position)."
msgstr ""

#. callout item
msgctxt "idex-alignment/prepare"
msgid ""
"Other firmwares (Marlin `M218`, the Prusa XL's nozzle offsets) also apply "
"their offsets themselves. Use the slicer's **Extruder offset** only if your "
"firmware does not."
msgstr ""

#. callout item
msgctxt "idex-alignment/prepare"
msgid ""
"Never \"fix\" a result by changing the slicer while the firmware also "
"applies an offset."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/prepare"
msgid ""
"Load T0 and T1 with the **same filament in two contrasting colours**, both "
"hot and primed."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/prepare"
msgid ""
"Set **skirt loops to 0**, turn off the **wipe tower** and **ooze "
"prevention**, and print in **normal** IDEX mode."
msgstr ""

#. point (warn)
msgctxt "idex-alignment/prepare"
msgid ""
"**No skew correction for this test.** A skew profile changes where the "
"printer puts every point, and a wrong one distorts the results: it can look "
"like a mechanical fault, or hide one. On Klipper, `SET_SKEW CLEAR=1` turns "
"it off ([Klipper: skew "
"correction](https://www.klipper3d.org/Skew_Correction.html)). **On RatOS** "
"a console command is not enough: `START_PRINT` and VAOC load the profile "
"named by `variable_skew_profile` in `[gcode_macro RatOS]` again ([RatOS "
"util.cfg](https://github.com/Rat-OS/RatOS-configuration/blob/v2.1.x/macros/util.cfg#L384-L396)). Comment that line out in `printer.cfg` for the test, and restore it afterwards."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/prepare"
msgid ""
"**Two contrasting colours**, same filament type: load T0 and T1 with, for "
"example, orange and blue, or black and white. The reading depends on "
"telling T0 from T1 at a glance. The reference photos are **PETG**; PLA "
"works just as well."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/prepare"
msgid ""
"**Both tools loaded, hot and primed.** Use the temperatures you normally "
"print with."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/prepare"
msgid ""
"**Skirt loops = 0** (Print Settings › Skirt and brim › **Loops "
"(minimum)**). The skirt would go around the whole layout: about 1.5 m of "
"filament per loop on a 400 mm bed, sometimes one loop per tool."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/prepare"
msgid ""
"**No wipe tower, no ooze prevention** (Print Settings › Multiple "
"Extruders). The test needs neither: there is only one tool change. **No "
"brim** is already forced on the object."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/prepare"
msgid ""
"**IDEX mode: normal** (single toolhead, automatic tool change), not "
"**copy** or **mirror**. RatOS does not apply `idex_xoffset` in copy or "
"mirror mode."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/prepare"
msgid ""
"**A good first layer:** bed mesh applied, and the **Z offset between the "
"tools** already set (most multi-tool printers measure it automatically, for "
"example RatOS with its Z probe and VAOC, the Prusa XL tool offset "
"calibration, the Bambu Lab H2D nozzle offset calibration, the Snapmaker J1 "
"Z offset calibration; otherwise set it by hand as your printer's manual "
"describes). A T1 line that is squished or starved changes the line width: "
"the Vernier does not care, but the Lite does."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/prepare"
msgid ""
"**Elephant foot compensation is forced to 0 on the object**, whatever your "
"profile says. The whole test is one first layer: the compensation would "
"shrink every line by its value (often 0.1–0.2 mm), eat the one-line ticks "
"and shift every contact edge of the Lite. Do not set it back in the "
"object's settings."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "idex-alignment/prepare"
msgid ""
"The other settings forced on the object are there for the same reason, a "
"clean single layer: Arachne perimeters (each tick becomes one bead), no "
"supports, no raft, no ironing, no fuzzy skin."
msgstr ""

#. More: photo alt
msgctxt "idex-alignment/prepare"
msgid ""
"RatOS dashboard after VAOC: the nozzle camera view, the toolhead panel and "
"the console reporting the IDEX toolhead offset applied for T0, then a "
"perfect toolhead z-offset of 0.005 mm for T1"
msgstr ""

#. More: photo caption
msgctxt "idex-alignment/prepare"
msgid ""
"Example, RatOS: VAOC measures the Z offset between the tools and reports it "
"in the console (here 0.005 mm for T1). Other printers have their own "
"routine."
msgstr ""

#. part heading (groups steps)
msgctxt "idex-alignment"
msgid "Print it"
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "idex-alignment/open-project"
msgid "Open it as a project"
msgstr ""

#. callout title
msgctxt "idex-alignment/open-project"
msgid "Before you print: one object, two extruders"
msgstr ""

#. callout
msgctxt "idex-alignment/open-project"
msgid ""
"After opening, the **object list** on the right must show **one object** "
"whose parts are named after the stations, assigned to **extruder 1** and "
"**extruder 2**."
msgstr ""

#. callout item
msgctxt "idex-alignment/open-project"
msgid ""
"Every station has two parts, for example `S1 back-left: T0 main scales` "
"(extruder 1) and `S1 back-left: T1 vernier scales` (extruder 2). Lite: `T0 "
"outer U` and `T1 inner U`."
msgstr ""

#. callout item
msgctxt "idex-alignment/open-project"
msgid ""
"**Everything on extruder 1, or a pile of separate objects: do not print.** "
"Open the file again with **File › Open project**, not Import."
msgstr ""

#. callout item
msgctxt "idex-alignment/open-project"
msgid ""
"Do not move, scale, rotate or arrange single parts. The card knows where "
"each station sits **relative to the bed centre**."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/open-project"
msgid ""
"In PrusaSlicer, **File › Open project** (Ctrl+O, or ⌘O on macOS) and choose "
"the .3mf."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/open-project"
msgid ""
"Choose your **IDEX (multi-extruder) printer profile** and a filament for "
"each extruder."
msgstr ""

#. point (warn)
msgctxt "idex-alignment/open-project"
msgid ""
"Opening the project replaces anything already on the plate. Print the test "
"**on its own**."
msgstr ""

#. photo alt
msgctxt "idex-alignment/open-project"
msgid ""
"PrusaSlicer object list of the IDEX Vernier test, with station parts on "
"extruders 1 and 2"
msgstr ""

#. photo caption
msgctxt "idex-alignment/open-project"
msgid ""
"One object; each station has a T0 part on extruder 1 and a T1 part on "
"extruder 2."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/open-project"
msgid ""
"If you drag the file onto the window instead, PrusaSlicer asks what to do: "
"choose **Open as project**. **Import 3D models only** drops the extruder "
"assignment."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/open-project"
msgid ""
"The project carries **no printer, filament or print profile**: PrusaSlicer "
"keeps yours. Choose your **IDEX (multi-extruder) printer profile** and a "
"filament for each extruder."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/open-project"
msgid ""
"PrusaSlicer **centres** the test on your bed. The stations end up about 10 "
"mm from the edges for the matching preset."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "idex-alignment/check"
msgid "Check the slice"
msgstr ""

#. callout title
msgctxt "idex-alignment/check"
msgid "One layer, one tool change"
msgstr ""

#. callout
msgctxt "idex-alignment/check"
msgid "Slice and look at the **Preview** before printing."
msgstr ""

#. callout item
msgctxt "idex-alignment/check"
msgid ""
"The vertical slider shows **exactly one layer**. Any first-layer height "
"from 0.1 to 0.39 mm gives one layer."
msgstr ""

#. callout item
msgctxt "idex-alignment/check"
msgid ""
"In the legend, switch the view to **Tool**: the T0 parts in one colour, the "
"T1 parts in the other, at every station."
msgstr ""

#. callout item
msgctxt "idex-alignment/check"
msgid ""
"The G-code has **one tool change**: all of T0 first, then all of T1. Export "
"it and search for your tool-change command (for example `T1`): one hit in "
"the print, besides any in the start G-code."
msgstr ""

#. callout item
msgctxt "idex-alignment/check"
msgid ""
"**No skirt** around the layout. If there is one, set the loops to 0 ([step "
"3](page:idex-alignment#prepare))."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/check"
msgid "Click **Slice now** and switch to **Preview**."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/check"
msgid ""
"Zoom on a Vernier station: each tick must show as **one single bead**, with "
"no tick missing. Lite: the outer and inner U are separate lines that touch."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/check"
msgid ""
"Check the object's settings (the gear icon next to the object in the list): "
"**elephant foot compensation 0**."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/check"
msgid ""
"Pick **your** printer, print and filament profiles in the right-hand panel, "
"as for any print."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "idex-alignment/check"
msgid ""
"PrusaSlicer orders the tools of a layer to start with the last one used, so "
"a single-layer print with two extruders gets one tool change. With "
"`toolchange_ordering = optimized` the order is T0 then T1."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "idex-alignment/print"
msgid "Print it"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/print"
msgid ""
"Send it and start: a **single layer**, **under 0.5 g** for the Lite and "
"**under 2 g** for the Vernier, done in **minutes**."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/print"
msgid ""
"Watch T1's first station: if its line is thin or missing, prime T1 and "
"print again."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/print"
msgid "**Leave the print on the bed** and read it there."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/print"
msgid ""
"Watch the first station of T1: if it starts with a thin or missing line, T1 "
"was not primed. Prime it in your tool-change macro and print again."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/print"
msgid ""
"**Leave the print on the bed.** Read it there, before removing it. Peeling "
"a single layer stretches it, and PETG on textured PEI hardly comes off flat."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "idex-alignment/print"
msgid ""
"Write the date and the current tool offsets on a piece of paper next to the "
"printer. You will compare the next test with this one."
msgstr ""

#. part heading (groups steps)
msgctxt "idex-alignment"
msgid "Read it"
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "idex-alignment/stations"
msgid "Find the stations"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/stations"
msgid ""
"Stations are numbered like a **phone keypad seen from the front**: 1 "
"back-left, 5 centre, 9 front-right."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/stations"
msgid ""
"**Read on the bed**, standing at the front, so left, right, front and back "
"are the printer's. If you must turn your head, the badge tells you which "
"way is up."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/stations"
msgid ""
"Use a **10× loupe** or a phone's macro mode, with **raking light** from one "
"side."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/stations"
msgid ""
"Stations are numbered like a **phone keypad seen from the front of the "
"printer**: **1** back-left, **2** back, **3** back-right, **4** left, **5** "
"centre, **6** right, **7** front-left, **8** front, **9** front-right. Beds "
"under 300 mm have only 1, 3, 5, 7 and 9."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/stations"
msgid ""
"Each station has a **hexagonal badge** with its number, printed by T0. The "
"number reads upright when you stand at the front."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "idex-alignment/stations"
msgid ""
"A **10× loupe** or the **macro mode** of a phone makes the 0.05 mm steps "
"easy. Use **raking light**: a lamp low and to the side, skimming across the "
"lines, so each line casts a small shadow. Light from above makes them "
"disappear into the plate."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "idex-alignment/stations"
msgid ""
"Read one station at a time and write X and Y down at once, with the station "
"number. Nine stations make 18 numbers: easy to mix up."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid "Read the Lite: gap or overlap"
msgstr ""

#. step intro
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid ""
"T0 prints the outer U, T1 the inner U. The **vertical U** reads **X**; the "
"**horizontal U** reads **Y**."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid "Rule of thumb: **T1 moved away from the gap, towards the overlap.**"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid ""
"Compare the stations. **The same answer everywhere** is a plain offset. "
"**An answer that changes from station to station** is mechanics: print the "
"Vernier and use the card."
msgstr ""

#. photo alt
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid "Plan of one Lite station: two nested U shapes and the hexagonal badge"
msgstr ""

#. photo caption
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid ""
"One Lite station: the vertical U reads X, the horizontal U reads Y. T0 in "
"orange, T1 in blue."
msgstr ""

#. table header
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid "What you see"
msgstr ""

#. table header
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid "Meaning"
msgstr ""

#. table header
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid "On the card"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid "Both contact lines look the same, no gap and no ridge"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid "Aligned here (within about 0.1 mm)"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid "Vertical U: **gap on the left**, overlap or ridge on the right"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid "T1 sits to the **right** (+X) of T0"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid "X: +"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid "Vertical U: gap on the right, overlap on the left"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid "T1 sits to the **left** (−X)"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid "X: −"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid "Horizontal U: **gap at the front**, overlap at the back"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid "T1 sits towards the **back** (+Y)"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid "Y: +"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid "Horizontal U: gap at the back, overlap at the front"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid "T1 sits towards the **front** (−Y)"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid "Y: −"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid "Gap on **both** sides, or overlap on both"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid "Not an offset: T1's line is thinner (or wider) than modelled"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid "0, and see the note below"
msgstr ""

#. More: intro
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid ""
"Each Lite station has two nested U shapes. T0 prints the outer U, T1 the "
"inner U, and T1's outer edges are modelled to **touch** T0's inner edges "
"exactly. The **vertical U** (rails front to back) reads **X**; the "
"**horizontal U** (rails left to right) reads **Y**."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid ""
"**A gap on both sides** is a width problem, not an offset: T1 "
"under-extrudes, its first layer is too high, or the Z offset between the "
"tools is off. An overlap on both sides is the opposite. Fix the first layer "
"of T1 before trusting the Lite."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid ""
"On the card, Lite readings count as ±0.1 mm. The fit then shows which "
"mechanism changes the sign across the bed, but no offset to apply."
msgstr ""

#. More: photo alt
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid ""
"Close-up of a Lite station with a gap on the left side of the vertical U"
msgstr ""

#. More: photo caption
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid ""
"Gap on the left, overlap on the right: T1 sits to the right of T0 here."
msgstr ""

#. More: stand-in photo alt
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid ""
"The Lite 400 printed on the bed, seen from the front: nine small stations "
"in yellow and red"
msgstr ""

#. More: stand-in photo caption
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid ""
"The author's Lite 400 on the bed (T0 yellow, T1 red). A close-up of a "
"station with a gap is still to come."
msgstr ""

#. More: photo alt
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid "Close-up of an aligned Lite station: both contact lines look the same"
msgstr ""

#. More: photo caption
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-lite"
msgid "Aligned: both contact lines look the same, no gap and no ridge."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-vernier"
msgid "Read the Vernier: the fine scale"
msgstr ""

#. step intro
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-vernier"
msgid ""
"Each station has an **X block** on the left and a **Y block** on the right, "
"each with a **fine** and a **coarse** scale."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-vernier"
msgid "**Find the T1 tick that lines up best** with the T0 tick next to it."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-vernier"
msgid ""
"**Read the label next to it**, in tenths of a millimetre (\"1\" = 0.10 mm); "
"the unlabelled tick just beyond a label adds 0.05."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-vernier"
msgid ""
"**Sign:** positive on the **\"+\"** side, **right** in the X block and "
"**back** in the Y block."
msgstr ""

#. photo alt
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-vernier"
msgid "Plan of one Vernier station: X block, hexagonal badge, Y block"
msgstr ""

#. photo caption
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-vernier"
msgid ""
"One Vernier station. X block on the left, Y block on the right; in each, "
"the fine scale (labels to 5) and the coarse scale (labels to 10). The \"+\" "
"marks the positive end."
msgstr ""

#. photo alt
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-vernier"
msgid ""
"Photo of a printed Vernier station: on the left the X block, in the middle "
"the hexagonal badge with the number 8, on the right the Y block; T0 in "
"yellow with the labels, T1 in red"
msgstr ""

#. photo caption
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-vernier"
msgid ""
"The same station printed, station 8 of the Vernier 400: T0 yellow, T1 red. "
"Each red comb sits right next to the yellow scale it is read against."
msgstr ""

#. More: intro
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-vernier"
msgid ""
"Each Vernier station has an **X block** on the left (ticks standing front "
"to back, spread left to right) and a **Y block** on the right (ticks lying "
"left to right, spread front to back), with the badge in between. Each block "
"has a **fine** scale (labels up to 5) and a **coarse** scale (labels up to "
"10)."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-vernier"
msgid ""
"T0 prints the **main scale** and its labels; T1 prints the **vernier "
"scale** right next to it, with slightly closer ticks. In the X block the "
"labels are below T0's comb; in the Y block, to its right."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-vernier"
msgid ""
"**Find the T1 tick that lines up best with the T0 tick next to it**, as if "
"they were one straight line. Around it, the neighbouring ticks are off by "
"the same amount in opposite directions, like an arrowhead pointing at the "
"right tick."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-vernier"
msgid ""
"**Read the number next to that T0 tick.** The labels are in **tenths of a "
"millimetre**: \"1\" = 0.10 mm, \"3\" = 0.30 mm. Only every second tick has "
"a label: the **unlabelled tick just beyond a label adds 0.05**. One tick "
"past the \"2\", away from 0, reads 0.25."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-vernier"
msgid ""
"**The sign:** a **\"+\"** marks the positive end of each scale, **right** "
"in the X block and **back** in the Y block. A reading on the \"+\" side is "
"positive, on the other side negative. Aligned on \"0\": reading 0.00."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-vernier"
msgid ""
"Example: in the X block the best tick is the first unlabelled one past "
"\"1\", on the \"+\" side: **X = +0.15**. T1 prints 0.15 mm to the right of "
"T0 there."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-vernier"
msgid ""
"Write X and Y for every station, for example `5: X +0.15, Y −0.05`, and "
"type them into the [card](page:idex-alignment-scorecard)."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-vernier"
msgid ""
"If two neighbouring ticks look equally good, the value is halfway between "
"them (0.025 steps). That is optional and not yet verified at a 0.4 mm "
"nozzle: the scale is rated at 0.05 mm."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-vernier"
msgid ""
"Why it works: T0's fine ticks are 1.10 mm apart, T1's 1.05 mm. If T1 prints "
"d mm to the right, its tick k sits at k × 1.05 + d and meets T0's tick k "
"(at k × 1.10) when k = d ÷ 0.05. The label is the offset."
msgstr ""

#. More: photo alt
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-vernier"
msgid ""
"Close-up drawing of the fine X scale with T1 printed 0.15 mm to the right"
msgstr ""

#. More: photo caption
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-vernier"
msgid ""
"T1 printed +0.15 mm: the blue tick one past the \"1\", on the right, "
"continues the orange tick below it. Its neighbours are off on both sides by "
"the same amount."
msgstr ""

#. More: photo alt
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-vernier"
msgid "Macro photo of a fine Vernier scale aligned on 0"
msgstr ""

#. More: photo caption
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-vernier"
msgid "Reading 0.00: the ticks at \"0\" form one straight line."
msgstr ""

#. More: stand-in photo alt
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-vernier"
msgid ""
"Close-up of the fine X scale of a printed Vernier station: the red T1 comb "
"above the yellow T0 scale labelled 5 to 0 to 5"
msgstr ""

#. More: stand-in photo caption
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-vernier"
msgid ""
"The fine X scale of station 8 of the author's print, close up (T0 yellow, "
"T1 red). A photo of a scale reading 0.00 is still to come."
msgstr ""

#. More: photo alt
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-vernier"
msgid ""
"Macro photo of the fine X scale of station 5 reading -0.30: an amber arrow "
"marks the red T1 tick that lines up with the yellow T0 tick at the \"3\" on "
"the left"
msgstr ""

#. More: photo caption
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-vernier"
msgid ""
"Reading -0.30, from the author's print with idex_xoffset raised by 0.25 on "
"purpose: the aligned tick is the \"3\" on the side away from the \"+\", so "
"T1 printed 0.30 mm to the left of T0. The neighbours drift apart on both "
"sides."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-coarse"
msgid "When to use the coarse scale"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-coarse"
msgid ""
"**Look at the coarse scale first.** Find the T1 tick that continues a T0 "
"tick. If it is **0, 2 or 4** (up to 0.4 mm), read the fine scale and leave "
"the coarse box empty."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-coarse"
msgid ""
"If it is **6, 8 or 10**, the offset is large. Either apply the coarse "
"value, print again and read the fine scale, or type **both** readings into "
"the card: it works out the true value."
msgstr ""

#. photo alt
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-coarse"
msgid ""
"Two simulated X blocks side by side. Left, T1 printed 0.20 mm to the right: "
"the coarse scale lines up at 2 and the fine scale at 2, which reads +0.20. "
"Right, T1 printed 0.60 mm to the right: the coarse scale lines up at 6, "
"while the fine scale seems to line up near its far negative end, about −0.45"
msgstr ""

#. photo caption
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-coarse"
msgid ""
"Simulation. Left, a small offset (T1 +0.20 mm): the coarse scale lines up "
"at 2, so read the fine scale: +0.20. Right, a large offset (T1 +0.60 mm): "
"the coarse scale lines up at 6, and the fine scale seems to read −0.45 at "
"its far end because it has wrapped around."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-coarse"
msgid ""
"The fine scale covers ±0.50 mm, and it **repeats**: past 0.5 mm it seems to "
"line up again near the other end. The coarse scale (0.2 mm steps, ±1.0 mm) "
"tells you which window you are in."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-coarse"
msgid ""
"Also use it when the fine scale **lines up nowhere**, or only at its last "
"tick (\"5\"): you are out of its range."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-coarse"
msgid ""
"Offsets above 1 mm are out of range of both scales. Correct roughly first "
"(VAOC, or a single-spot test), then use this one."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-mistakes"
msgid "Common misreadings"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-mistakes"
msgid ""
"Find the **\"+\"** first, and judge a tick by the **symmetry of its "
"neighbours**, not on its own."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-mistakes"
msgid ""
"Print with **skew correction off**, and with a first layer that is not "
"over-squished."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-mistakes"
msgid ""
"**A tick merged by squish:** when the first layer is too low, neighbouring "
"ticks spread and touch, and a blob looks like an alignment. Judge by the "
"**symmetry of the neighbours**, not by one tick. If whole rows of ticks "
"merge, raise T1's first layer (or the Z offset between the tools) and print "
"again."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-mistakes"
msgid ""
"**Elephant foot:** a wide, squashed first layer makes every tick fatter and "
"closes the gaps between them. The Vernier still reads the centres, but the "
"alignment becomes hard to see. On the Lite, it closes the gaps on both "
"sides. Check that the object's elephant foot compensation is 0 and the "
"first layer is not over-squished."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-mistakes"
msgid ""
"**Reading the wrong side:** the labels carry no sign. Always find the "
"**\"+\"** first: right in the X block, back in the Y block. Standing at the "
"side of the printer, or looking at a photo turned around, flips the sign."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-mistakes"
msgid ""
"**Mixing up the blocks:** the X block is the one with ticks standing front "
"to back. The Y block, right of the badge, has ticks lying left to right."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-mistakes"
msgid ""
"**Reading the coarse scale as fine:** the coarse labels go to 10 and step "
"by 2 (2 = 0.20 mm); the fine labels go to 5 and step by 1."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-mistakes"
msgid ""
"**A skew profile was active:** a wrong `[skew_correction]` profile bends "
"the results across the bed and sends you looking for a mechanical fault "
"that is not there. One user lost most of a tuning session this way; with "
"the skew set to 0 the readings made sense. Print this test with skew "
"correction off ([step 3](page:idex-alignment#prepare)), and measure the "
"skew again only **after** the IDEX mechanics are fixed."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (tip)
msgctxt "idex-alignment/read-mistakes"
msgid ""
"Not sure? Photograph the scale with the phone, zoom in, and count the ticks "
"from \"0\" to the aligned one: each tick is 0.05 mm on the fine scale, 0.2 "
"mm on the coarse."
msgstr ""

#. part heading (groups steps)
msgctxt "idex-alignment"
msgid "Apply the result"
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "idex-alignment/scorecard"
msgid "Enter the readings in the card"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/scorecard"
msgid ""
"Open the [IDEX alignment card](page:idex-alignment-scorecard). Choose the "
"**bed preset** of the file you printed and the test (Vernier or Lite), then "
"type X and Y for each station. Fill the coarse boxes only when you read the "
"coarse scale."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/scorecard"
msgid ""
"**Fix the mechanics before the offset.** With a slope marked **fix**, no "
"single offset is right everywhere. Correct the cause ([next "
"step](page:idex-alignment#diagnosis)), print again, then set the offset."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/scorecard"
msgid ""
"The card fits a plane through the readings of each axis, **reading = c0 + "
"cx·x + cy·y**, with x and y measured from the bed centre."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/scorecard"
msgid ""
"It reports the **offset at the bed centre** (c0 for X and Y), and a verdict "
"for each slope, judged by its effect at the outer stations: **ok** below "
"0.05 mm, **watch** from 0.05 to 0.10 mm, **fix** above 0.10 mm. It also "
"shows rotation and shear, and flags any station more than 0.10 mm off the "
"plane."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/scorecard"
msgid ""
"With your current offsets typed in, it prints the exact RatOS "
"`SAVE_VARIABLE` lines, plus the PrusaSlicer, Prusa XL and Marlin "
"equivalents."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "idex-alignment/scorecard"
msgid ""
"You can also print the card and write the numbers by hand: the rules are on "
"page 1, the grid on page 2."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "idex-alignment/diagnosis"
msgid "What the pattern means"
msgstr ""

#. step intro
msgctxt "idex-alignment/diagnosis"
msgid ""
"Each pattern points to one cause. \"X reading\" is the number from the X "
"blocks, \"Y reading\" from the Y blocks."
msgstr ""

#. table header
msgctxt "idex-alignment/diagnosis"
msgid "Pattern on the bed"
msgstr ""

#. table header
msgctxt "idex-alignment/diagnosis"
msgid "Likely cause"
msgstr ""

#. table header
msgctxt "idex-alignment/diagnosis"
msgid "What to do and check"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/diagnosis"
msgid "**Same reading everywhere** (offset only)"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/diagnosis"
msgid "A plain tool offset"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/diagnosis"
msgid ""
"Set the offset ([next step](page:idex-alignment#apply)). Nothing mechanical "
"to check."
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/diagnosis"
msgid "**X reading grows from left to right**"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/diagnosis"
msgid "Carriage X scale: the two carriages travel different distances in X"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/diagnosis"
msgid ""
"Check both X belts (same tension, same belt), the pulley set screws and the "
"motor mounts. If T0 measures right on a dimensional test, set T1's "
"`rotation_distance` to old × (1 + slope)."
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/diagnosis"
msgid "**Y reading changes from left to right**"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/diagnosis"
msgid ""
"Rails not parallel, or gantry twist: T1's X travel is not parallel to T0's"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/diagnosis"
msgid ""
"Check that the X rail(s) are straight and bolted down along their whole "
"length, and that the gantry is not twisted. A pure rotation can also be a "
"loose toolhead mount. **RatRig V-Core 4/4.1 IDEX:** see the belt-length "
"note below."
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/diagnosis"
msgid "**X reading changes from front to back**"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/diagnosis"
msgid "Racking or yaw of the gantry, seen differently by the two carriages"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/diagnosis"
msgid ""
"Square the gantry on both Y sides, compare the left and right Y belt "
"tension, check the gantry joints."
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/diagnosis"
msgid "**Y reading changes from front to back**"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/diagnosis"
msgid ""
"Rare: a Y scale difference, usually a misreading, a loose hotend or a "
"moving bed"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/diagnosis"
msgid ""
"Re-read; check that both nozzles and hotends are tight and the bed does not "
"move. **RatRig V-Core 4/4.1 IDEX:** the belt-length note below may apply "
"too."
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/diagnosis"
msgid "**One station off** (more than 0.1 mm from the others' plane)"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/diagnosis"
msgid "A misreading, a bad first layer there (bed mesh), or local backlash"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/diagnosis"
msgid ""
"Re-read that station with a loupe; if it persists, check the bed mesh and "
"the carriage at that spot."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "idex-alignment/diagnosis"
msgid ""
"\"Y reading changes from left to right\" means the **T1 lines drift further "
"from T0 the further you go** across the bed: for example Y −0.10 at the "
"left stations, 0.00 in the middle, +0.10 at the right. A fixed offset "
"cannot fix that."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (tip)
msgctxt "idex-alignment/diagnosis"
msgid ""
"**RatRig V-Core 4/4.1 IDEX, belt length (field experience, the author's own "
"printer):** a Y gap between T0 and T1 that grew across the bed was fixed by "
"shortening **one toolhead's belt by 1–2 teeth**, so that the two toolhead "
"belts were the **same length**. Count the teeth, or compare the belt ends "
"at the clamps of the two toolheads: a difference of 1–2 teeth is enough to "
"cause it. Then **re-tension both equally** and print the Vernier again to "
"confirm. RatRig's build guide asks for **identical lengths for the two Y "
"belts** ([V-Core 4.1 build guide, step "
"42](https://wiki.ratrig.com/products/v-core-4-1/build-guide/x-axis-and-belt-installation); [V-Core 4.0 IDEX Y belts](https://wiki.ratrig.com/build-guides/v-core-40-hybrid-and-idex-y-axis-belts)); we found no official note on the toolhead belts. Treat it as a tip to check, not a rule."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/diagnosis"
msgid ""
"**Rotation and shear** (expert): the card also combines the two cross "
"slopes. A **rotation** means T1's frame is turned against T0's (the "
"toolhead or a carriage is mounted at a slight angle); a **shear** means the "
"axes are not square to each other in the same way for both tools."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "idex-alignment/diagnosis"
msgid ""
"The slope is in mm per mm: an X slope of +0.0005 means T1 travels 0.05 % "
"farther than T0, about +0.1 mm at 200 mm from the centre."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "idex-alignment/belt-iterations"
msgid "Equalise the belts step by step"
msgstr ""

#. step intro
msgctxt "idex-alignment/belt-iterations"
msgid ""
"Belts suspected? Change one thing at a time and measure after each run."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/belt-iterations"
msgid ""
"**Move one belt anchor by one tooth**, then re-tension both belts the same "
"way."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/belt-iterations"
msgid ""
"**Redo the offset calibration** (VAOC on RatOS), then **print the test "
"again**."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/belt-iterations"
msgid ""
"**Compare** with the previous run in the "
"[card](page:idex-alignment-scorecard): smaller is better; if it grew, go "
"back one tooth."
msgstr ""

#. More: intro
msgctxt "idex-alignment/belt-iterations"
msgid ""
"When the Y reading changes across the bed (`dy_dx`, sometimes `dy_dy`) and "
"the belts are the suspect, go one small change at a time and measure after "
"each. Another RatRig V-Core 4 IDEX user reached about 0.05 mm this way, by "
"moving only the belt anchoring at the toolheads, a little in or out each "
"time."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/belt-iterations"
msgid ""
"**Move one anchor by one tooth.** Shift the belt end in one toolhead clamp "
"by one tooth, in or out. Change one thing per run."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/belt-iterations"
msgid ""
"**Re-tension** both belts the same way as before (same frequency or the "
"same method you always use)."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/belt-iterations"
msgid ""
"**Redo the offset calibration:** VAOC on RatOS, or your printer's Z and XY "
"offset routine. A belt change moves the nozzle, so the old offsets no "
"longer hold."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/belt-iterations"
msgid ""
"**Print the test again.** The **Lite** is enough to see whether you are "
"going the right way; print the **Vernier** for the numbers once it looks "
"close."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/belt-iterations"
msgid ""
"**Compare with the previous run:** type the readings into the "
"[card](page:idex-alignment-scorecard), and compare the **Y left to right** "
"and **Y front to back** effects with the run before. Smaller is better; if "
"it grew, go back one tooth and try the other side."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/belt-iterations"
msgid ""
"**Keep a log** like the table above, on paper or in the card (**Remember "
"this run** keeps the previous result in your browser and shows it next to "
"the new one; **Copy results** puts the text on the clipboard)."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "idex-alignment/belt-iterations"
msgid ""
"Write down which clamp you moved and in which direction. After three or "
"four runs nobody remembers."
msgstr ""

#. More: table header
msgctxt "idex-alignment/belt-iterations"
msgid "Run"
msgstr ""

#. More: table header
msgctxt "idex-alignment/belt-iterations"
msgid "Change made"
msgstr ""

#. More: table header
msgctxt "idex-alignment/belt-iterations"
msgid "Y left to right (`dy_dx`)"
msgstr ""

#. More: table header
msgctxt "idex-alignment/belt-iterations"
msgid "Y front to back (`dy_dy`)"
msgstr ""

#. More: table header
msgctxt "idex-alignment/belt-iterations"
msgid "Offset X / Y"
msgstr ""

#. More: table header
msgctxt "idex-alignment/belt-iterations"
msgid "Notes"
msgstr ""

#. More: table cell
msgctxt "idex-alignment/belt-iterations"
msgid "none (starting point)"
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "idex-alignment/apply"
msgid "Set the offset"
msgstr ""

#. step intro
msgctxt "idex-alignment/apply"
msgid ""
"On every system: **new value = old value + reading**, X and Y separately, "
"using the offset at the bed centre from the card."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/apply"
msgid ""
"**RatOS:** type the two `SAVE_VARIABLE` lines the card writes "
"(`idex_xoffset`, `idex_yoffset`) in the console."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/apply"
msgid ""
"**PrusaSlicer, Prusa XL, Marlin:** add the readings to the stored offset; "
"the card writes the values."
msgstr ""

#. point (warn)
msgctxt "idex-alignment/apply"
msgid ""
"**RatOS with `default_toolhead = 1`:** the roles of the two tools swap, and "
"the rule becomes **new = old − reading**. This is derived from the RatOS "
"macros and **not tested on a printer**: do the sign check below."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/apply"
msgid ""
"Print the Vernier again: the readings should now be within ±0.05 mm at "
"every station."
msgstr ""

#. More: intro
msgctxt "idex-alignment/apply"
msgid ""
"**Reading** = where T1 prints minus where T0 prints, for the same commanded "
"position. +X is to the right, +Y towards the back, seen from the front. On "
"every system below: **new value = old value + reading**, X and Y "
"separately, using the offset at the bed centre from the card."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/apply"
msgid ""
"**RatOS** (Klipper IDEX, `default_toolhead = 0`): `idex_xoffset` += X and "
"`idex_yoffset` += Y. Type in the console `SAVE_VARIABLE "
"VARIABLE=idex_xoffset VALUE=<new>` and `SAVE_VARIABLE VARIABLE=idex_yoffset "
"VALUE=<new>`. The card writes both lines when you give it the current "
"values, which are in `ratos-variables.cfg`. Restart Klipper "
"(`FIRMWARE_RESTART`) afterwards so that every macro reads the new values."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/apply"
msgid ""
"Example: `idex_xoffset` is 0.30 and the card says X +0.15: the new value is "
"0.45. `idex_yoffset` is −0.12 and Y reads −0.05: the new value is −0.17."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/apply"
msgid ""
"**PrusaSlicer** (only if the firmware does not apply offsets): Printer "
"Settings › **Extruder 2** › Position › **Extruder offset**: add the "
"readings to X and Y. RatOS users keep this at 0, 0."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/apply"
msgid ""
"**Prusa XL:** in the printer's toolhead settings, the **nozzle offset** of "
"the tested tool: add the readings (0.01 mm steps, ±1 mm). Re-running the "
"automatic calibration overwrites manual changes."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/apply"
msgid ""
"**Marlin** (`M218`): `M218 T1 X<old + X> Y<old + Y>`, then `M500` to store "
"it. On dual X carriage machines `M218 T1 X` is the X2 home position: the "
"same rule applies."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "idex-alignment/apply"
msgid ""
"Why \"+\": on RatOS the non-default toolhead runs with `SET_GCODE_OFFSET "
"X_ADJUST={-idex_xoffset}`, so a larger `idex_xoffset` moves T1 towards −X. "
"PrusaSlicer subtracts `extruder_offset` from the G-code coordinates, Marlin "
"and the XL subtract the hotend offset from the commanded position. All "
"three move T1 the opposite way of the offset, so adding the reading cancels "
"it."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "idex-alignment/sign-check"
msgid "Check the sign once (required with RatOS)"
msgstr ""

#. callout title
msgctxt "idex-alignment/sign-check"
msgid "Required the first time you use the test with RatOS"
msgstr ""

#. callout
msgctxt "idex-alignment/sign-check"
msgid ""
"The sign rule comes from the firmware source code. A deliberate change of "
"0.20 mm proves it on your machine in two short prints. Recommended on any "
"other firmware too."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/sign-check"
msgid ""
"Read **station 5, X** (call it **a**), add **+0.20** to `idex_xoffset`, and "
"print the Vernier again."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/sign-check"
msgid ""
"It must now read **a − 0.20**. If it reads **a + 0.20**, the sign is "
"reversed: use **new = old − reading**, and please tell us."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/sign-check"
msgid "Then set `idex_xoffset` to the value the card suggests."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/sign-check"
msgid ""
"Print the Vernier and read **station 5, X**: call it **a** (for example "
"+0.15)."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/sign-check"
msgid ""
"Add **+0.20** to `idex_xoffset`: `SAVE_VARIABLE VARIABLE=idex_xoffset "
"VALUE=<current + 0.20>`."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/sign-check"
msgid ""
"Print the Vernier again and read station 5, X. It must now read **a − "
"0.20** (in the example, −0.05): the reading **decreases by 0.20**."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/sign-check"
msgid ""
"If it reads **a + 0.20** instead, the sign is reversed on your machine: use "
"**new = old − reading**, and please tell us (with your RatOS version and "
"`default_toolhead`)."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "idex-alignment/when-again"
msgid "When to run it again"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/when-again"
msgid ""
"After **VAOC** or any automatic offset calibration, to confirm it over the "
"whole bed."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/when-again"
msgid ""
"After changing a **nozzle**, a **hotend** or a **toolhead**, or taking a "
"toolhead off its carriage."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/when-again"
msgid ""
"After work on **belts, pulleys, rails or the gantry**, or after a crash."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/when-again"
msgid ""
"When two-colour parts show a step where the colours meet, or supports in "
"the second tool land beside the part."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/when-again"
msgid ""
"A quick **Lite** every few months shows whether anything moved; the "
"**Vernier** when it did."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "idex-alignment/share"
msgid "Share your result"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/share"
msgid ""
"Printed the test? **Post your Make** on the [Printables "
"page](link:printables-idex-alignment) with a photo of the **whole bed** and "
"a close-up of your **best and your worst** station, and say which offset "
"you set."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/share"
msgid ""
"Makes help other users judge their own print, and they help the project "
"grow."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/share"
msgid ""
"We regularly pick the clearest Makes and ask their authors whether we may "
"use the photo in this official guide, with their name and a link."
msgstr ""

#. part heading (groups steps)
msgctxt "idex-alignment"
msgid "Troubleshooting"
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "idex-alignment/troubleshooting"
msgid "The slice or the print looks wrong"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**Everything prints with one tool, or the stations are separate objects:** "
"the file was imported, or came from a ZIP. Open the single .3mf with **File "
"› Open project**."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**A skirt circles the whole bed:** set Print Settings › Skirt and brim › "
"**Loops (minimum)** to 0."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**Two layers instead of one:** the first-layer height is 0.4 mm or more. "
"Use 0.1 to 0.39 mm for this print."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**A wipe tower appears:** turn it off in Print Settings › Multiple "
"Extruders. The test does not need it."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**Stations too close to the edge, or off the bed:** the preset is for a "
"bigger bed. Use the one for your bed size, or a smaller one."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**Ticks missing or merged in the Preview:** check the object's settings "
"(elephant foot compensation 0, Arachne) and that the object was not scaled."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**T1's first station is thin or missing:** T1 was not primed after the tool "
"change. Prime it in the tool-change macro and print again."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "idex-alignment/troubleshooting-more"
msgid "The readings make no sense"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**The fine scale lines up nowhere, or at its last tick:** the offset is "
"beyond ±0.5 mm. Read [the coarse scale](page:idex-alignment#read-coarse)."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**The reading changes across the bed:** that is mechanics, not offset. See "
"[what the pattern means](page:idex-alignment#diagnosis)."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**After applying the correction the error doubled:** the sign is reversed "
"on your setup (or the offset is set in both the firmware and the slicer). "
"Do the [sign check](page:idex-alignment#sign-check) and set the slicer's "
"extruder offset to 0 on RatOS."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**The card says coarse and fine disagree:** one of the two is misread. "
"Re-read both, starting from the \"+\" end."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**Gap or overlap on both sides of the Lite:** a line width problem, not an "
"offset. Check T1's flow and first-layer height."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**The Y gap grows across the bed on a RatRig V-Core 4/4.1 IDEX:** check "
"that the two toolhead belts are the same length. Count the teeth, or "
"compare the belt ends at the clamps; 1–2 teeth of difference are enough. "
"Re-tension both equally and print the Vernier again (field experience, the "
"author's own printer)."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**The readings change across the bed in a way no belt or rail explains, or "
"change after a restart:** check that no skew correction was active ([step "
"3](page:idex-alignment#prepare)). On RatOS look for `variable_skew_profile` "
"in `[gcode_macro RatOS]`: `START_PRINT` loads it even after a `SET_SKEW "
"CLEAR=1` in the console."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**Nothing changes after `SAVE_VARIABLE`:** the printer is in copy or mirror "
"mode, where RatOS does not apply `idex_xoffset`, or the value is not "
"reloaded yet. Print in normal mode."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "idex-alignment/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**One station far off the others:** re-read it with a loupe; then check the "
"bed mesh and the first layer at that spot."
msgstr ""

#. page title
msgctxt "license"
msgid "License and credits"
msgstr ""

#. page description
msgctxt "license"
msgid ""
"How the suite's models, guides and code are licensed, the reserved name, "
"and the sources the tests build on."
msgstr ""

#. page intro
msgctxt "license"
msgid ""
"{suite} is open: you may use, share and adapt the models, the guides and "
"the code under the licenses below. What is reserved is the **name**.\n"
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "license/licenses"
msgid "Licenses"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "license/licenses"
msgid ""
"CC BY-SA 4.0 means you may share and adapt the models and texts, also "
"commercially, as long as you **give credit** and **share your changes under "
"the same license**."
msgstr ""

#. point
