#. More: intro
msgctxt "max-flow/which-file"
msgid ""
"Nine files in four tiers. The tier comes from your hotend, the file from "
"your nozzle. Each file's top band is at least **120 % of the highest "
"claim** of its tier, so a hotend that really reaches its claim still shows "
"a failing band."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/which-file"
msgid ""
"**Claims are not measurements.** Makers state their figure with different "
"filaments, temperatures, nozzles (often 0.8–1.2 mm) and failure criteria; "
"on a 0.4 nozzle every hotend reaches far less. **≈** marks a figure we "
"found only through a retailer or a search excerpt, not on the maker's own "
"page: treat it as approximate."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/which-file"
msgid ""
"Times are estimates at 4000 mm/s²; PrusaSlicer shows the real time after "
"slicing. Grams are PETG, 1.75 mm filament. The footprint includes the 3 mm "
"brim."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/which-file"
msgid ""
"**Seven wide bands on the bigger files.** `hf-0.6`, `uhf-0.6`, `uhf-0.8` "
"and both `extreme` files go up in larger steps (8, 12 and 25 mm³/s, about "
"12–14 % of the top band) to save a third or more of the filament. Each band "
"is as tall as on the other files, so it is just as easy to judge; the limit "
"is simply less precise ([what that changes](page:max-flow#choose))."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/which-file"
msgid ""
"**Bed fit.** Every file fits a 180 × 180 mm bed (Prusa MINI, Bambu Lab A1 "
"mini) with room for a skirt, except `uhf-0.4`: it is 193 mm long, because "
"holding 336 mm/s for 0.3 s needs it. It fits a MK4 (250 × 210), a Core One "
"(250 × 220) and any larger bed."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/which-file"
msgid ""
"**Nothing failed?** Print the next tier for your nozzle (standard → hf → "
"uhf → extreme). **Everything failed?** Print the tier below."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/which-file"
msgid ""
"**IDEX and multi-tool:** `max-flow_hf-0.4_10-50_T0.3mf` / `_T1.3mf` and "
"`max-flow_uhf-0.4_16-64_T0.3mf` / `_T1.3mf` are assigned to that tool, "
"object and speed codes. Per-tool versions of the other files are not "
"published yet."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "max-flow/which-file"
msgid ""
"**Your own range:** `python3 -I calib.py max-flow --start 6 --end 30 --step "
"3 --nozzle 0.4 --name PETG-revo`. Add `--estimate` for grams, time, top "
"speed and the acceleration the top band needs."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "max-flow/which-file"
msgid ""
"**Worked example, the author's RatRig V-Core 4 IDEX** (Klipper): Phaetus "
"Rapido UHF and an LDO Orbiter 2.5 on both tools; T0 has an \"Alpha\" PCD "
"0.4 nozzle, T1 a Phaetus PS M6 copper 0.4. Files: "
"`max-flow_hf-0.4_10-50_T0.3mf` and `_T1.3mf`. **T0, PETG at 240 °C:** band "
"30 is the last clean wall, band 34 the onset; the heater held within ±0.8 "
"°C (72 % power at most) and the extruder never clicked, so the hotend's "
"melt was the limit, not the extruder. Set **25 mm³/s** (85 %). T1 has the "
"same V6 bore: expect a band or two either way, and give it its own number."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "max-flow/which-file"
msgid ""
"**Taking the 0.6 or 0.8 file for a 0.4 nozzle because the hotend is UHF.** "
"A 0.4 nozzle caps even a UHF hotend: Ellis measured about 30 mm³/s on a "
"Rapido UHF with a 0.4 brass nozzle against the 75 claimed. Pick the file "
"for the nozzle you print with."
msgstr ""

