#. step title
msgctxt "max-flow/safety"
msgid "Temperature and safety"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "max-flow/safety"
msgid ""
"Print at the temperature you will print with: the one from the [temperature "
"tower](page:temperature-tower)."
msgstr ""

#. point (warn)
msgctxt "max-flow/safety"
msgid ""
"**Stay below your hotend's maximum temperature** (and the filament's). "
"Never raise it, or a firmware limit, to chase a higher band."
msgstr ""

#. point (warn)
msgctxt "max-flow/safety"
msgid ""
"**Hotends with a PTFE (Teflon) tube down to the nozzle are not safe above "
"about 250 °C:** the tube degrades and gives off harmful fumes."
msgstr ""

#. point (warn)
msgctxt "max-flow/safety"
msgid ""
"**Watch the hotend temperature graph during the top bands.** A heater that "
"cannot keep up sags; Klipper may stop the print with \"Heater extruder not "
"heating at expected rate\"."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/safety"
msgid ""
"Max flow rises steeply with temperature (CNC Kitchen: a V6 that skipped at "
"15 mm³/s at 215 °C ran 15 mm³/s cleanly at 275 °C). **If you change the "
"temperature later, the result is no longer valid: test again.**"
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/safety"
msgid ""
"At 50–180 mm³/s the heater melts several grams per minute; a 40–60 W "
"cartridge can fall behind. A sag of more than a few °C is itself a failure "
"of that band ([heater sag](page:max-flow#heater))."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/safety"
msgid ""
"The uhf and extreme files move at up to 336 mm/s: check that the toolhead, "
"the belts and the part's grip on the bed are fine at that speed before "
"leaving it alone."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "max-flow/print"
msgid "Slice and print"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "max-flow/print"
msgid ""
"Start the print and **stay with it**: listen to the extruder and watch the "
"hotend temperature during the upper bands."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "max-flow/print"
msgid ""
"Note the number on the column at the nozzle height when the extruder clicks "
"or the temperature sags."
msgstr ""

#. point (warn)
msgctxt "max-flow/print"
msgid ""
"If the extruder grinds hard for several bands, stop the print and send "
"`M220 S100`."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/print"
msgid ""
"A thin, wavy or broken line on the straights, seen at the nozzle, is the "
"onset. The printer's console also shows the band being printed (`;CALIB "
"max_flow band=N`)."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/print"
msgid ""
"Let the part cool before taking it off. Keep the brim on until you have "
"read it: it holds the thin wall straight."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "max-flow/print"
msgid ""
"Write the filament, the temperature, the nozzle and the tool on a piece of "
"tape on the brim."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "max-flow/print"
msgid ""
"**Nozzle-cam recording:** `python3 -I calib.py record --test max-flow "
"--printer <printer URL> --camera <camera name>` follows the print and "
"records one clip per band over its upper two thirds "
"(`max-flow_wall_band8_18mm3s.mp4`). Compare a clean band with the onset "
"band side by side."
msgstr ""

#. More: photo alt
msgctxt "max-flow/print"
msgid ""
"Two nozzle-cam clips side by side, same framing, half speed, about one "
"layer each: on the left band 6 (30 mm³/s), an even, closed wall; on the "
"right band 8 (38 mm³/s), dark gaps between the lines"
msgstr ""

#. More: photo caption
msgctxt "max-flow/print"
msgid ""
"Nozzle cam, the author's PETG on T0 at 240 °C, half speed. Left, band 6, 30 "
"mm³/s: the last clean wall. Right, band 8, 38 mm³/s: dark gaps on the "
"straights. The heater held within ±0.8 °C and the extruder never clicked: "
"the hotend's melt was the limit."
msgstr ""

#. part heading (groups steps)
msgctxt "max-flow"
msgid "Read it"
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "max-flow/how-to-read"
msgid "How to read it"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "max-flow/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"Read **from the bottom up**, on the **middle of the two long straights, "
"both walls**, in the **upper two thirds** of each band."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "max-flow/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"On the [scorecard](page:max-flow-scorecard), mark the wall of each band "
"**✓**, **◐** (degrading), **✕** (fail) or **–**, and tick **Heater sag** "
"and **Clicks** where you noted them."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "max-flow/how-to-read"
msgid "Do the squeeze test **last**."
msgstr ""

#. photo alt
msgctxt "max-flow/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"Top view of the flow test: the hex column on the left, the straight along "
"X, the straight at 30° and the round end"
msgstr ""

#. photo caption
msgctxt "max-flow/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"Seen from above: the column (numbers on its front-left face, seam on its "
"rear corner), the two long straights and the round end."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"**The numbers** are on the column's front-left face, which is doubled "
"(thicker): the flow of each band in mm³/s. The **seam** is on the column's "
"rear corner, away from the straights you read."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"**Both walls:** the 30° straight faces the front, like the numbers; the "
"straight along X faces the back. Turn the part round, or look at it from "
"inside the wedge. They must agree."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"**Primary signals:** the long straights, the see-through test, extruder "
"clicks and heater sag. Any one of them failing makes the band fail. The "
"sheen, the column and the squeeze test confirm and explain."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"The steps below show what each looks like. Then [choose the "
"value](page:max-flow#choose); the on-screen card does it for you."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "max-flow/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"**Judging the column or the round end.** The nozzle nearly stops at the "
"column's corners and slows in the round end on fast bands, so they look "
"good longer than the straights. Read the middle of the straights."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "max-flow/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"**Reading the transition layers.** The first layers of each band are still "
"settling at the new flow; judge above them."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "max-flow/choose"
msgid "Choose the value"
msgstr ""

#. step intro
msgctxt "max-flow/choose"
msgid "The on-screen card does this for you."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "max-flow/choose"
msgid ""
"The **onset** is the lowest band that fails (wall ✕, light through it, "
"clicks or heater sag). The **limit** is the band just below it."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "max-flow/choose"
msgid ""
"Use **80–90 % of the limit**: 85 % as a rule, 80 % if the limit band was "
"already degrading."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "max-flow/choose"
msgid "**IDEX:** each tool gets its own result."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/choose"
msgid ""
"Example, the author's PETG on hf-0.4 (T0, PCD 0.4 nozzle, 240 °C): band 30 "
"is clean, band 34 shows the first gaps, the heater stays within ±0.8 °C and "
"the extruder is silent. Onset 34, limit **30**: the hotend's melt capacity. "
"**25 mm³/s** (85 %)."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/choose"
msgid ""
"Example, standard-0.4: band 18 fails, band 16 is clean. Limit 16: **13–14 "
"mm³/s**."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/choose"
msgid ""
"**Band 1 already fails:** not a flow problem. Check the temperature, a "
"partial clog, wet filament and the filament diameter in the profile."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/choose"
msgid ""
"**Nothing fails:** print the next file up for your nozzle, or a custom "
"range above the top band."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/choose"
msgid ""
"**Seven-band files** (`hf-0.6`, `uhf-0.6`, `uhf-0.8`, `extreme`): one step "
"is 12–14 % of the top band, so your real limit lies somewhere between the "
"last clean band and the onset. 80–90 % of the last clean band is still a "
"safe setting: you only leave part of one step unused. Example, PETG on "
"uhf-0.6: band 64 is clean, band 76 shows gaps. Limit 64: **51–58 mm³/s**."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/choose"
msgid ""
"The jump between two wide bands is large, so a **degrading** band is rarer "
"and the loss of gloss may show up on the onset band itself. Judge each band "
"just as on the other files."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "max-flow/choose"
msgid ""
"**Fine pass, when the last mm³/s matter:** print 7 narrow bands centred "
"midway between your last clean band and the onset, same file family: "
"`python3 -I calib.py max-flow --preset uhf-0.6 --centre 70` gives "
"`max-flow_uhf-0.6_fine_58-82.3mf` (steps of 3 on hf-0.6, 4 on uhf, 8 on "
"extreme). It costs about as much filament as the first file (14–40 g), so "
"print it only if you need the precision. Read it the same way; on the "
"scorecard, type its flows as a custom range. `--tool 1` works here too."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/choose"
msgid ""
"**IDEX:** T0 and T1 may differ by a band or two, even with identical "
"hotends: nozzles, extruders and thermistors differ. Write both down and use "
"each on its own tool."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "max-flow/choose"
msgid ""
"**Using the onset band itself, or 100 % of the limit.** The limit is the "
"edge of a cliff: real prints have longer moves, bridges and infill that "
"heat the hotend differently. The 10–20 % margin keeps them clear."
msgstr ""

#. part heading (groups steps)
msgctxt "max-flow"
msgid "Apply the result"
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "max-flow/apply"
msgid "Put it in your filament profile"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "max-flow/apply"
msgid ""
"**Filament Settings › Advanced › Max volumetric speed** = your value. Save "
"the profile, e.g. `Brand PETG – 240 °C – 25`."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "max-flow/apply"
msgid ""
"Leave **Print Settings › Speed › Autospeed (advanced) › Max volumetric "
"speed** at **0**."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "max-flow/apply"
msgid ""
"**IDEX:** one filament profile per tool, each with its own value, selected "
"on its extruder."
msgstr ""

#. photo alt
msgctxt "max-flow/apply"
msgid ""
"PrusaSlicer filament settings, Advanced page, the Max volumetric speed "
"field set to 25 mm³/s"
msgstr ""

#. photo caption
msgctxt "max-flow/apply"
msgid "Filament Settings › Advanced: Max volumetric speed, here 25 mm³/s."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/apply"
msgid ""
"This is the **one change you keep**. PrusaSlicer then slows down any move "
"that would need more plastic than this."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/apply"
msgid ""
"The print-wide **Autospeed (advanced) › Max volumetric speed** is a second "
"cap that applies to every filament and every tool: keep it at 0 (off), or "
"at least at the filament value."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/apply"
msgid ""
"**IDEX and multi-tool:** PrusaSlicer has no per-extruder max volumetric "
"speed in the printer settings; each extruder uses its own filament profile. "
"Make one per tool (for example `PETG T0 ALPHA 0.4` and `PETG T1 PS 0.4`) "
"and select each on its extruder in the plater."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/apply"
msgid ""
"The value belongs to this filament **at this temperature**, with this "
"hotend, nozzle and extruder. **After any temperature change, test again.**"
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/apply"
msgid ""
"Then re-check **pressure advance**: it depends on the flow you print at."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "max-flow/when-again"
msgid "When to run it again"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "max-flow/when-again"
msgid "A new filament, or a new printing temperature for it."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "max-flow/when-again"
msgid "A new hotend, heater, nozzle (size or material) or extruder."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/when-again"
msgid ""
"Two spools of the same brand and type usually agree; a different colour can "
"differ by a band."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/when-again"
msgid "Then, in the sequence: **pressure advance**, then **flow**."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "max-flow/air"
msgid "Optional: the extrude-in-air check"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "max-flow/air"
msgid ""
"For a precise number without a tower, or to confirm a borderline band: "
"extrude a fixed length of filament in the air at each flow and **weigh** "
"(or measure) what came out."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "max-flow/air"
msgid ""
"The tower's result is the one to use for printing: it is usually a little "
"lower."
msgstr ""

#. More: intro
msgctxt "max-flow/air"
msgid ""
"In the printer's console: `M83` (relative extrusion), a short prime, then "
"one `G1 E45 F…` per flow: a 2 mm³/s reference, then each band of your file. "
"With 1.75 mm filament, F is the flow × 24.95 (2 mm³/s: `F50`; 20 mm³/s: "
"`F499`). Pause after each step to cut the blob. There is no X or Y move."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/air"
msgid ""
"Heat the hotend to your printing temperature and **park the nozzle at least "
"50 mm above the bed**."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/air"
msgid ""
"Send the commands one step at a time. After each step, cut the blob with "
"tweezers and put it on a numbered paper."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/air"
msgid ""
"**Weigh it** (a 0.01 g scale): each blob should weigh **0.137 g** in PETG "
"(45 mm of 1.75 mm filament). Efficiency = blob ÷ the 2 mm³/s reference "
"blob. The limit is the last flow above about **97 %**."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/air"
msgid ""
"**Or measure it** (Ellis' method): before each step, mark the filament 65 "
"mm above the extruder inlet; after it, 20 mm should remain. More than 21 mm "
"left means more than 2 % lost."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/air"
msgid ""
"45 mm per step stays under Klipper's `max_extrude_only_distance` (50 mm by "
"default) and Marlin's `EXTRUDE_MAXLENGTH`. Klipper's "
"`max_extrude_only_velocity` and Marlin's `M203 E` cap these steps."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "max-flow/air"
msgid "At UHF flows a 45 mm step lasts under 2 s: prefer the tower there."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "max-flow/share"
msgid "Share your result"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "max-flow/share"
msgid ""
"Printed the test? **Post your Make** on the [Printables "
"page](link:printables-max-flow) with a photo of your **last good and first "
"failed** band, your hotend, nozzle, temperature and the value you chose."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/share"
msgid ""
"Makes help other users judge their own print, and they help the project "
"grow. Results from hotends not in the table are especially welcome."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/share"
msgid ""
"We regularly pick the clearest Makes and ask their authors whether we may "
"use the photo in this official guide, with their name and a link."
msgstr ""

#. part heading (groups steps)
msgctxt "max-flow"
msgid "Troubleshooting"
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "max-flow/troubleshooting"
msgid "The bands do not get faster"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "max-flow/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**The tower printed at a crawl, no markers on the slider:** open the single "
".3mf with **File › Open project**, not Import."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "max-flow/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**The upper bands all look the same, too good:** a firmware limit capped "
"them ([check](page:max-flow#firmware-limits))."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "max-flow/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**The next print runs far too fast:** send `M220 S100` (Klipper: or "
"`RESTART`)."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**The legend shows lower flows than the engraved numbers on the "
"straights:** your filament's cooling slowdown or max volumetric speed "
"capped the base speed; see [the session-only "
"fix](page:max-flow#open-project)."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**The time shown is several times too long and the legend shows the slow "
"base speed (5–17 mm/s):** a RepRapFirmware printer profile. The printer "
"still prints the real bands."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**The top bands stop getting faster on a printer that accepts high factors "
"badly** (above 999 %): generate the file with `--max-factor 999`."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**Klipper stops with \"Move exceeds maximum extrusion\":** "
"`max_extrude_cross_section` was lowered below its default. The tower needs "
"0.30 × nozzle²."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "max-flow/troubleshooting-more"
msgid "Other problems"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "max-flow/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**Band 1 already fails:** too cold, a partial clog, wet filament, or the "
"wrong filament diameter."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "max-flow/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**The column fails with the straights:** the extruder is skipping or "
"something slows the whole layer; not a melt limit."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "max-flow/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**Klipper: \"Heater extruder not heating at expected rate\":** the heater "
"could not keep up. The band where it stopped is above your limit."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**The thin wall came loose or bent at speed:** keep the brim, clean the "
"bed, check the first layer. A wobbly wall makes the straights hard to read."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**PrusaSlicer says the object is outside the print area:** the `uhf-0.4` "
"file is 193 mm long; use a bed of 200 mm or more. On a 180 mm bed, turn off "
"the skirt or move it closer, and keep the long side along X."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**Clicks in every band, from the start:** extruder tension, a worn gear, or "
"a bowden tube with too much friction. Fix that first."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**IDEX: the other tool heats or prints:** the object is assigned to the "
"wrong extruder. Use the `_T0` / `_T1` file for the tool you test, or set "
"the object's extruder."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "max-flow/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**PrusaSlicer 3.x:** these files are made for PrusaSlicer 2.9 (2.7 is the "
"minimum). For 3.x the tests will be prepared as Lua plugins, once its "
"plugin system is stable."
msgstr ""

#. page title
msgctxt "pressure-advance-scorecard"
msgid "Pressure advance scorecard"
msgstr ""

#. page description
msgctxt "pressure-advance-scorecard"
msgid ""
"A printable scoring card for the pressure advance test: pass, too low or "
"too high for each probe of each band."
msgstr ""

#. page intro
msgctxt "pressure-advance-scorecard"
msgid ""
"Choose your file in the firmware menu (or type the values of a custom "
"range), score each band on screen or on paper, and let the rule pick the "
"value. How to judge each probe: [pressure advance "
"guide](page:pressure-advance#how-to-read).\n"
"\n"
"Write down too: **firmware**, **nozzle**, filament **brand, type, colour**, "
"**temperature**, **test speed (98 mm/s) and acceleration**, **date**. A "
"value without them is useless next month.\n"
msgstr ""

#. page breadcrumb
msgctxt "pressure-advance-scorecard"
msgid "Printable card"
msgstr ""

#. callout
msgctxt "pressure-advance-scorecard"
msgid ""
"The columns follow the hex pinwheel model v1.3.0 (coarse and fine files are "
"both in the firmware menu). **90° tips**, **45° tip** and **Straights** "
"decide; **Seam** and **120°** only confirm; **Inside** is shown but **not "
"counted** (a concave corner is rounded by the bead width at any PA). "
"Printed on A4: the rules on page 1, the whole grid on page 2."
msgstr ""

#. page title
msgctxt "pressure-advance"
msgid "Pressure advance"
msgstr ""

#. page description
msgctxt "pressure-advance"
msgid ""
"Tune pressure advance (linear advance) per filament with a small hexagonal "
"tower: one PA value per band, scored band by band."
msgstr ""

#. page intro
msgctxt "pressure-advance"
msgid ""
"A small hexagonal tower printed in **bands of 4.6 mm**, each with its own "
"pressure advance value. Score the blade tips band by band, pick the winner "
"with a fixed rule, and type one number into your setup.\n"
msgstr ""

#. More: intro
msgctxt "pressure-advance"
msgid ""
"A small hollow hexagon with three blades, printed in **bands of 4.6 mm**. "
"Each band has its own pressure advance value, and the band number is "
"engraved on the front. The blades end in sharp 45° and 90° tips after long "
"straights, the hardest corners a printer meets. Too little PA makes them "
"bulge, too much makes them dull. You score each band, pick the winner with "
"a fixed rule, and type one number into your setup. There are two files per "
"firmware: a **coarse** one with a wide range, and a **fine** one with small "
"steps.\n"
"\n"
"The tower is 43 mm tall, uses about 4.5 g of filament and prints in about "
"14 minutes, with **no changes to your profiles**: the file sets the test "
"speed itself. The time was measured on the author's printer, the grams are "
"our estimate; PrusaSlicer shows the figures for your profiles after "
"slicing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. callout
msgctxt "pressure-advance"
msgid ""
"Open the file with **File › Open project**, never with **Import**: an "
"import keeps the shape but drops the PA changes and the test speed."
msgstr ""

#. box title
msgctxt "pressure-advance"
msgid "What you need"
msgstr ""

#. box column title
msgctxt "pressure-advance"
msgid "To print"
msgstr ""

#. box item
msgctxt "pressure-advance"
msgid ""
"The [file for your firmware and extruder](page:pressure-advance#download)"
msgstr ""

#. box item
msgctxt "pressure-advance"
msgid "PrusaSlicer 2.9 (2.7 is the minimum that opens the project)"
msgstr ""

#. box item
msgctxt "pressure-advance"
msgid "Your own printer, print and filament profiles, already working"
msgstr ""

#. box item
msgctxt "pressure-advance"
msgid ""
"The nozzle temperature found with the [temperature "
"tower](page:temperature-tower)"
msgstr ""

#. box item
msgctxt "pressure-advance"
msgid "The filament to test, **dry**, about 5 g of it"
msgstr ""

#. box column title
msgctxt "pressure-advance"
msgid "To read it"
msgstr ""

#. box item
msgctxt "pressure-advance"
msgid "A lamp you can move, and a dark card or cloth"
msgstr ""

#. box item
msgctxt "pressure-advance"
msgid ""
"The [scorecard](page:pressure-advance-scorecard), printed or on screen"
msgstr ""

#. box item
msgctxt "pressure-advance"
msgid "Optional: a phone camera, to zoom on the tips"
msgstr ""

#. part heading (groups steps)
msgctxt "pressure-advance"
msgid "Before printing"
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "pressure-advance/what-for"
msgid "What it is for, and when to run it"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/what-for"
msgid ""
"Find the **pressure advance** (PA) value: sharp corners, no bulges, no gaps "
"after them."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/what-for"
msgid ""
"Run it **after the temperature tower**, once **per filament** and once "
"**per toolhead**."
msgstr ""

#. photo alt
msgctxt "pressure-advance/what-for"
msgid ""
"The whole coarse pressure advance tower in yellow PETG, front view: band "
"numbers 1 to 9 engraved on the front face, band 1 at the bottom; the "
"outline of the right tip turns ragged in the top bands"
msgstr ""

#. photo caption
msgctxt "pressure-advance/what-for"
msgid ""
"The tower: band 1 (PA 0) at the bottom, more PA in each band going up. The "
"band numbers are engraved on the front. On this coarse tower (0 → 0.200) "
"the outline of the right tip, at the right edge, turns ragged in the top "
"bands: far too much PA."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/what-for"
msgid ""
"It finds the **pressure advance** (PA) value that keeps the line width even "
"when the nozzle slows down and speeds up: sharp corners without bulges, and "
"no gaps after them."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "pressure-advance/what-for"
msgid ""
"Molten plastic in the nozzle behaves a little like toothpaste in a tube: "
"when you squeeze, it takes a moment to come out, and when you stop, it "
"keeps oozing. When the nozzle brakes into a corner, plastic keeps coming "
"and piles up: the corner **bulges**. After the corner the flow arrives late "
"and the line is thin. PA tells the firmware to push a little earlier and "
"ease off a little earlier. Too much of it and the corners come out "
"**rounded**, dull or with small gaps."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/what-for"
msgid ""
"Klipper, Prusa and RepRapFirmware call it **pressure advance**; Marlin "
"calls it **linear advance** (`K` factor). It is the same idea, but the "
"numbers are not interchangeable between firmwares."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/what-for"
msgid ""
"Run it **after the temperature tower**: PA depends on how runny the plastic "
"is, so tune it at the temperature you will print with."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/what-for"
msgid ""
"Run it **per filament**: every type, brand and sometimes colour needs its "
"own value. And **per toolhead** on IDEX and multi-tool printers: each "
"hotend and extruder gets its own tower and its own value."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/what-for"
msgid ""
"In the sequence it comes after maximum volumetric flow and **before** flow "
"and retraction: good PA changes top surfaces and often removes most "
"stringing by itself."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "pressure-advance/what-for"
msgid ""
"The result is valid for the test speed (98 mm/s, 8 mm³/s) and the "
"external-perimeter acceleration of your profile, and it assumes input "
"shaping is already tuned. Write both down with the value."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "pressure-advance/coarse-or-fine"
msgid "Coarse or fine: which file first"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/coarse-or-fine"
msgid "Print the **fine** file if you already have a PA value to start from."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/coarse-or-fine"
msgid ""
"Print the **coarse** file if you know nothing about this filament or "
"printer, then a fine pass around its best band."
msgstr ""

#. More: intro
msgctxt "pressure-advance/coarse-or-fine"
msgid ""
"Every firmware and extruder has two files, both with 9 bands and the same "
"height. Print the coarse one **only if you do not know your PA at all**."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/coarse-or-fine"
msgid ""
"**Fine** (the usual choice): the range most printers and filaments land in, "
"in small steps (0.01 on Klipper and Prusa direct drive). If you have a "
"value from your filament profile or an earlier test, start here."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/coarse-or-fine"
msgid ""
"**Coarse:** a range two to five times wider, sure to reach **clearly too "
"high** in its top bands (0 → 0.20 on Klipper and Prusa direct drive). Print "
"it for a new filament or printer you know nothing about, or to see once, "
"plainly, what both too low and too high look like."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/coarse-or-fine"
msgid ""
"**Then a fine pass around the best coarse band:** print the **fine** file "
"for your firmware and extruder. If the best coarse band lies above the fine "
"file's range, take that band's value: the coarse result is your value."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/coarse-or-fine"
msgid ""
"**What too high looks like** at the top of a coarse tower: the tips are "
"**rounded or \"eaten\"**, the wall **thins just before each tip** (the last "
"1–2 mm of the straight look starved), and **gaps open at the line start** "
"on the seam bump at the back."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "pressure-advance/coarse-or-fine"
msgid ""
"`--centre` uses the preset's fine step; add `--step` for another one, e.g. "
"a quarter of it for a very fine third pass. Files are named like "
"`pressure-advance_klipper-dd_fine_0.01-0.09.3mf`."
msgstr ""

#. step title
