#. More: intro
msgctxt "pressure-advance/download"
msgid ""
"There is one pair of files (coarse and fine) per **firmware family** and "
"**extruder type**: PrusaSlicer pastes the PA command into the G-code "
"exactly as written, so each firmware needs its own file. Every file has 9 "
"bands; band 1 is always PA 0."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/download"
msgid ""
"The files are named like `pressure-advance_klipper-dd_coarse_0-0.20.3mf` "
"and `pressure-advance_klipper-dd_fine_0-0.08.3mf`: firmware and extruder, "
"**coarse or fine**, then the PA of the bottom and top bands. The value of "
"every band is also in the slicer: see [the "
"markers](page:pressure-advance#check)."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/download"
msgid ""
"Download the **single .3mf file**, not \"Download all\" as a ZIP: "
"PrusaSlicer opens a ZIP's 3MF as plain geometry and the PA changes are lost."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/download"
msgid ""
"Every band is 4.6 mm tall, on a 0.6 mm floor, with a 0.8 mm cap on top: the "
"tower is 42.8 mm tall. About 4.5 g of PETG (0.45 g per band); the file "
"prints with 0.45 mm lines and 0.2 mm layers, and the grams assume 1.75 mm "
"filament and no skirt."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/download"
msgid ""
"The ranges are made for a **0.4 mm nozzle**. Bigger nozzles usually need "
"**less** PA (Prusa's own values at 0.6 mm are about 60 % of those at 0.4), "
"so the same files still cover them, at a coarser step."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "pressure-advance/download"
msgid ""
"**Direct drive or bowden?** Follow the filament from the spool. If the "
"extruder motor sits on the print head and pushes the filament straight into "
"the hotend, it is **direct drive** (Prusa MK4, Core One, XL, most Klipper "
"machines). If the motor is on the frame and pushes the filament through a "
"long PTFE tube to the head, it is **bowden** (Prusa MINI, Ender 3 and many "
"older printers)."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "pressure-advance/download"
msgid ""
"**Which firmware?** Prusa MK4, MK4S, MK3.9, MK3.5, Core One, XL and MINI "
"run **Buddy**. If you control the printer from Mainsail or Fluidd, it runs "
"**Klipper**. Duet boards run **RepRapFirmware**. Most other printers, and "
"the Prusa MK3/MK3S, run **Marlin**."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/download"
msgid ""
"**XL and other Buddy multi-tool printers:** `M572` has no tool number on "
"Buddy; it applies to the **active tool**, which is the one printing the "
"tower. So the same `buddy-dd` file works for every XL tool: assign the "
"object to the tool you want to tune ([IDEX and "
"multi-tool](page:pressure-advance#idex-apply))."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "pressure-advance/download"
msgid ""
"**A custom range** (fine pass, other nozzle, other tool) is one command in "
"the {suite} [repository](link:repository), with Python 3.11 and nothing "
"else: `python3 -I calib.py pressure-advance --preset klipper-dd --centre "
"0.04` (the fine pass around a value), or `python3 -I calib.py "
"pressure-advance --firmware klipper --start 0.02 --end 0.04 --step 0.0025 "
"--name PETG-fine`. `--firmware` is `klipper`, `buddy`, `marlin` or `rrf`; "
"at most 20 bands. Also: `--variant coarse` or `fine` for one preset file, "
"`--tool N` for IDEX, `--nozzle 0.6`, `--restore V` (sets PA back to V at "
"the end)."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "pressure-advance/download"
msgid ""
"**Test faster when you can:** PA effects grow with speed. Add `--flow 12` "
"(about 147 mm/s) or `--speed <your real external-perimeter speed>` to any "
"of these commands, staying below 0.8 × your filament's max volumetric speed "
"([the test speed](page:pressure-advance#speed)). Example: `python3 -I "
"calib.py pressure-advance --preset klipper-dd --variant coarse --flow 12 "
"--restore 0.04`."
msgstr ""

#. part heading (groups steps)
msgctxt "pressure-advance"
msgid "Print it"
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "pressure-advance/open-project"
msgid "Open it as a project"
msgstr ""

#. callout title
msgctxt "pressure-advance/open-project"
msgid "Before you print: one marker per band"
msgstr ""

#. callout
msgctxt "pressure-advance/open-project"
msgid ""
"After opening, slice and look at the vertical layer slider in the Preview: "
"it must show **one marker per band**, plus one at the top. Hovering a "
"marker shows the PA command, for example `;CALIB pressure_advance band=3/9 "
"pa=0.020`, `SET_PRESSURE_ADVANCE ADVANCE=0.020` and `M220 S1651`."
msgstr ""

#. callout item
msgctxt "pressure-advance/open-project"
msgid ""
"On multi-extruder printer profiles (IDEX, XL, MMU) there may be a short "
"cluster of markers per band. That is fine."
msgstr ""

#. callout item
msgctxt "pressure-advance/open-project"
msgid ""
"**No markers: do not print.** Open the file again with **File › Open "
"project**, and check in **Preferences** that the load project dialog is on "
"(see below)."
msgstr ""

#. callout item
msgctxt "pressure-advance/open-project"
msgid ""
"Quick check: export the G-code and search it for `;CALIB pressure_advance`: "
"[details in the next step](page:pressure-advance#check)."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/open-project"
msgid ""
"In PrusaSlicer, **File › Open project** (Ctrl+O, or ⌘O on macOS) and choose "
"the .3mf."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/open-project"
msgid ""
"Dragging the file in? Choose **Open as project**, never **Import 3D models "
"only**."
msgstr ""

#. point (warn)
msgctxt "pressure-advance/open-project"
msgid ""
"Opening the project replaces anything already on the plate. The PA changes "
"belong to the plate, so print the tower **on its own**."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/open-project"
msgid ""
"If you drag the file onto the window instead, PrusaSlicer asks what to do: "
"choose **Open as project**. **Import 3D models only** drops the PA changes."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/open-project"
msgid ""
"If you once ticked \"don't show again\" with the wrong choice, dragging "
"will import silently. Turn the question back on in **Preferences › General "
"› Show load project dialog**."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/open-project"
msgid ""
"The project carries **no printer, filament or print profile**: PrusaSlicer "
"keeps yours. It only carries the tower, a few per-object settings the test "
"needs (0.2 mm layers, 0.45 mm lines, two perimeters, both external, classic "
"perimeter generator, no gap fill, no infill, the seam at the back, the test "
"speed) and the PA changes."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "pressure-advance/open-project"
msgid ""
"Do not scale, rotate, cut or sink the tower. The PA changes are fixed "
"heights: if the tower changes size, they land in the wrong place."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "pressure-advance/check"
msgid "Check the markers"
msgstr ""

#. callout title
msgctxt "pressure-advance/check"
msgid "No markers, no print"
msgstr ""

#. callout
msgctxt "pressure-advance/check"
msgid ""
"The vertical slider must show **one marker per band**, at the first layer "
"of each band. Hovering a marker shows the PA command for that band."
msgstr ""

#. callout item
msgctxt "pressure-advance/check"
msgid ""
"On multi-extruder printer profiles (IDEX, XL, MMU) there may be more than "
"one marker per band. That is fine: they all carry the same value."
msgstr ""

#. callout item
msgctxt "pressure-advance/check"
msgid ""
"**If no markers are visible, do not print.** The PA changes were dropped "
"and the whole tower would print with one value. Open the file again with "
"**File › Open project** (not Import), and check **Preferences › General › "
"Show load project dialog** so that dragging a file asks you again."
msgstr ""

#. callout item
msgctxt "pressure-advance/check"
msgid ""
"Quick check before printing: export the G-code and search it for `;CALIB "
"pressure_advance`. All nine bands must be there, from `band=1/9 pa=0.000` "
"up."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/check"
msgid ""
"Select **your** profiles, click **Slice now** and switch to **Preview**."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/check"
msgid "Hover a marker: the PA value must go up one step per band."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/check"
msgid ""
"Check that the command matches your firmware: `SET_PRESSURE_ADVANCE` "
"(Klipper), `M572 S` (Prusa Buddy), `M900 K` (Marlin), `M572 D0 S` "
"(RepRapFirmware)."
msgstr ""

#. photo alt
msgctxt "pressure-advance/check"
msgid ""
"PrusaSlicer preview of the pressure advance tower with a marker per band on "
"the layer slider"
msgstr ""

#. photo caption
msgctxt "pressure-advance/check"
msgid "One marker per band; hover to read the PA command."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/check"
msgid ""
"Pick **your** printer, print and filament profiles in the right-hand panel, "
"as for any print."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/check"
msgid ""
"Click **Slice now** and switch to **Preview**. On the vertical layer slider "
"there is a small marker at the first layer of each band."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/check"
msgid ""
"Hover a marker: it shows the custom G-code, a comment such as `;CALIB "
"pressure_advance band=3/9 pa=0.020`, the command for your firmware and "
"`M220 S1651` (the test speed, [next step](page:pressure-advance#speed)). "
"The values must go up one step per band. The last marker, at the top, sets "
"`M220 S100` back."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/check"
msgid ""
"Check that the command matches your firmware: `SET_PRESSURE_ADVANCE` for "
"Klipper, `M572 S` for Prusa Buddy, `M900 K` for Marlin, `M572 D0 S` for "
"RepRapFirmware. A wrong family is ignored by the printer, or stops it with "
"an error."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "pressure-advance/check"
msgid ""
"The same line may appear more than once per band: on multi-extruder "
"profiles PrusaSlicer keeps a custom G-code only if it lands exactly on a "
"layer top, so each command is repeated on a 0.01 mm grid. That is harmless. "
"Unlike temperature, PA acts at once, so there is no transition zone: each "
"band is valid from its first layer."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "pressure-advance/speed"
msgid "The test speed: nothing to change"
msgstr ""

#. callout title
msgctxt "pressure-advance/speed"
msgid "If you cancel the print, send M220 S100"
msgstr ""

#. callout
msgctxt "pressure-advance/speed"
msgid ""
"The file speeds the printer up with `M220`, and the cap at the top sets it "
"back with `M220 S100`. If you **cancel** the print before the top, type "
"**`M220 S100`** in the printer's console (or terminal): Klipper, Marlin and "
"RepRapFirmware keep the factor, and your next print would run many times "
"too fast. Prusa Buddy printers (MK4, Core One, XL, MINI) reset it at the "
"next print start by themselves."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/speed"
msgid ""
"**No profile changes are needed.** The walls print at **98 mm/s**, about "
"**8 mm³/s**, with your own acceleration."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/speed"
msgid ""
"Check it in the Preview: switch the legend to **Speed**; the walls show "
"about **98 mm/s** in every band."
msgstr ""

#. photo alt
msgctxt "pressure-advance/speed"
msgid ""
"PrusaSlicer preview of the tower in Speed view, the walls at about 98 mm/s"
msgstr ""

#. photo caption
msgctxt "pressure-advance/speed"
msgid ""
"Preview, Speed view: the walls at about 98 mm/s from the bottom band to the "
"top, with your profiles unchanged."
msgstr ""

#. photo alt
msgctxt "pressure-advance/speed"
msgid ""
"PrusaSlicer preview of the same tower in Volumetric flow rate view, the "
"walls at about 7–8 mm³/s"
msgstr ""

#. photo caption
msgctxt "pressure-advance/speed"
msgid "The same preview, Volumetric flow rate: the walls at about 7–8 mm³/s."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/speed"
msgid ""
"How: the file asks PrusaSlicer for a very low wall speed (about 6 mm/s), so "
"the slicer sees long layers and never slows them down for cooling. Every "
"band also sends **`M220 S1651`**, a speed factor that the printer applies: "
"6 × 16.5 ≈ 98 mm/s. `M220` works on every firmware, so it adds no new file "
"variants."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/speed"
msgid ""
"The floor, the first layer and the 0.8 mm cap at the top print at **100 "
"%**, with your own speeds."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/speed"
msgid ""
"**Check in the Preview:** slice, and in the legend at the bottom left "
"switch the view from **Feature type** to **Speed**: the walls show about "
"**98 mm/s**, the same from the bottom band to the top. In **Volumetric flow "
"rate** they show about **8 mm³/s**. The print time is the real one. This "
"works with Klipper, Marlin and Prusa printer profiles: PrusaSlicer applies "
"`M220` in its preview."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/speed"
msgid ""
"**RepRapFirmware printer profiles:** PrusaSlicer ignores `M220` in the "
"preview there. The legend shows the low base speed and the time is many "
"times too long; the printer still prints at 98 mm/s."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/speed"
msgid ""
"**Fan:** the slicer sees long layers, so the fan runs at your filament's "
"**minimum** fan speed, with the bridge fan on the few short overhangs of "
"the engraved numbers. That is how your big parts are cooled."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "pressure-advance/speed"
msgid ""
"Why a fixed speed: PA only shows when the nozzle changes speed. Before, the "
"slicer slowed a small tower like this one down to 15 mm/s, where even PA 0 "
"looks fine."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "pressure-advance/speed"
msgid ""
"**Another test speed:** generate a file with `--speed <your "
"external-perimeter speed>` or `--flow <mm³/s>` ([custom "
"range](page:pressure-advance#download)). PrusaSlicer can no longer cap the "
"flow with your filament's **max volumetric speed**, so stay below it: use "
"about **0.8 × your filament's max volumetric speed** (with 15 mm³/s, "
"`--flow 12`)."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "pressure-advance/speed"
msgid ""
"If the Speed view shows less than 98 mm/s, your filament's **Slow down if "
"layer print time is below** is above 28 s, which is rare. Lower it for this "
"print only, and do not save the change."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "pressure-advance/settings"
msgid "Check the rest of the settings"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/settings"
msgid ""
"Print at the temperature you chose with the [temperature "
"tower](page:temperature-tower)."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/settings"
msgid ""
"Use the **print profile you really print with**, and write down its "
"external-perimeter acceleration."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/settings"
msgid "Make sure nothing else sets PA during the print."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/settings"
msgid ""
"Set the nozzle temperature to the one you chose with the [temperature "
"tower](page:temperature-tower). PA found at another temperature is not the "
"right one."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/settings"
msgid ""
"Use the **print profile you really print with**: the file sets the speed, "
"but the **external-perimeter acceleration** is your profile's. **Write it "
"down** with the test speed (on Klipper also `square_corner_velocity`): the "
"value is valid for them."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/settings"
msgid ""
"**No other PA source during the print.** The **Before / After layer change "
"G-code** must not set PA. A PA line in the filament start G-code is fine: "
"band 1's command comes later and wins."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/settings"
msgid ""
"**Sequential printing off** (it disables the per-layer commands), **spiral "
"vase off**, **variable layer height off**. The file sets 0.2 mm layers; any "
"first layer below 0.6 mm works."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/settings"
msgid "**Skirt** 0 or 1 loop. **Wipe tower** and **ooze prevention** off."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "pressure-advance/settings"
msgid ""
"Klipper's documentation tests at low acceleration "
"(`SQUARE_CORNER_VELOCITY=1 ACCEL=500`), which exaggerates the corners but "
"tunes PA for conditions you do not print at. If you want them, type the "
"command in the console before printing and `FIRMWARE_RESTART` afterwards: "
"the file itself sends no motion settings."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "pressure-advance/print"
msgid "Slice and print"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/print"
msgid ""
"Slice again after any change, export the G-code (or send it to the printer) "
"and start the print."
msgstr ""

#. point (warn)
msgctxt "pressure-advance/print"
msgid ""
"**After the print, the printer keeps the top band's PA** until something "
"sets it again. On the last layer the printer's screen shows **PA test done: "
"restore your PA**. On Klipper, `RESTART` or `FIRMWARE_RESTART` typed in the "
"console, or the next `SET_PRESSURE_ADVANCE`; on Marlin, a reset, and **do "
"not `M500`** before your own value is set; on Prusa Buddy, the next print's "
"filament start G-code sets it again."
msgstr ""

#. point (warn)
msgctxt "pressure-advance/print"
msgid ""
"**Close the dialog before you restart.** Mainsail rebuilds the window from "
"the console history when it reconnects, so after a `RESTART` typed with the "
"window still open, the window comes back. Click **Close** first, then type "
"`RESTART` once the print has finished: a restart during the print stops it."
msgstr ""

#. photo alt
msgctxt "pressure-advance/print"
msgid ""
"Mainsail showing the HexCalibr PA test finished dialog with its single "
"Close button"
msgstr ""

#. photo caption
msgctxt "pressure-advance/print"
msgid ""
"Klipper: on the last layer, Mainsail (or Fluidd) tells you once that the "
"top band's PA is still active. Close closes it."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/print"
msgid ""
"Watch the value change at each band. **Klipper:** every "
"`SET_PRESSURE_ADVANCE` answers in the Mainsail or Fluidd console with the "
"new `pressure_advance` value. **RepRapFirmware:** send `M572` with no value "
"in the console to read it. **Marlin:** many screens show the `K` value in "
"the advanced settings menu. **Prusa Buddy:** the screen does not show it; "
"follow the print height against the markers instead."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/print"
msgid ""
"The bands are short: a band takes about a minute. Watching the corner of "
"the right blade in the first bands, you can often see the bulge with your "
"own eyes."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/print"
msgid ""
"**Klipper: a dialog in Mainsail or Fluidd.** On the last layer, a few "
"seconds before the end, the Klipper files open a window once: **HexCalibr: "
"PA test finished**. It only informs you. The top band's PA is still active; "
"it goes back to your `printer.cfg` value after `RESTART` or "
"`FIRMWARE_RESTART`, typed in the console when the print has finished, or at "
"the next print if your filament start G-code sets PA. The only button, "
"**Close**, closes the window."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "pressure-advance/print"
msgid ""
"**One-click restore (optional).** Paste this macro into your `printer.cfg`, "
"save, and restart Klipper once: `\n"
"[gcode_macro HEXCALIBR_RESTORE]\n"
"gcode:\n"
"  RESPOND TYPE=command MSG=action:prompt_end\n"
"  RESTART\n"
"` After a test print, type `HEXCALIBR_RESTORE` in the console: it closes "
"the dialog, then reloads `printer.cfg`, with the `pressure_advance` of "
"every extruder and the speed factor at 100 %. The test files never run it: "
"you run it, when the print has finished."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "pressure-advance/print"
msgid ""
"The dialog uses Klipper's `RESPOND` command (`[respond]`, already in RatOS "
"and in the Mainsail and Fluidd configuration). Without it Klipper only "
"writes `Unknown command` in the console and the print goes on; Mainsail 2.9 "
"or later shows the window. The macro needs `[respond]` too."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "pressure-advance/print"
msgid ""
"Write the filament brand, colour, nozzle, test speed and acceleration on a "
"piece of tape on the tower. In a month you will not remember which tower "
"was which."
msgstr ""

#. part heading (groups steps)
msgctxt "pressure-advance"
msgid "Read it"
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "pressure-advance/how-to-read"
msgid "How to read it: score each band"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"Open the [scorecard](page:pressure-advance-scorecard) and choose your file "
"in the firmware menu."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"Hold the tower with the numbers facing you: band 1, at the bottom, is PA 0."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"Follow each **convex tip** from band 1 up under raking light, and mark ✓, "
"**L** (too low), **H** (too high) or **–** for each probe."
msgstr ""

#. photo alt
msgctxt "pressure-advance/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"Top view of one band of the pressure advance tower, the front face at the "
"bottom"
msgstr ""

#. photo caption
msgctxt "pressure-advance/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"One band seen from above, front (numbers, thicker wall) at the bottom. The "
"45° tip points to the bottom left, the right 90° tip to the right, the "
"back-left 90° tip to the top left; the seam bump is on the back face."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"Open the [scorecard](page:pressure-advance-scorecard) (print it, or fill it "
"on screen). One row per band, one column per probe. Choose your file in the "
"firmware menu: it fills in the PA value of every band."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"Hold the tower with the **engraved numbers facing you**. Band 1 is at the "
"**bottom** and is always **PA 0**. Each band's number is engraved on the "
"front face, above a groove that marks where the band starts."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"**Read the convex probes: PA shows there.** They are the **blade tips** "
"(the right and back-left 90° tips, the front-left 45° tip), the **last "
"millimetre of each straight** just before a tip, and the **seam bump** on "
"the back face. The inside corners where the blades fold back, and the three "
"plain **120°** corners of the hexagon, are only references."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"**Follow one convex corner from band 1 upward**, with the light raking "
"along the face. With PA too low its edge carries a vertical **row of small "
"blobs**; the row fades out at the right band. On the first real print "
"(PETG, Klipper, fine file) the blobs were there in bands 1–3 and gone from "
"band 4 (0.03) up."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"Use **raking light**: a lamp low and to the side, so the light skims along "
"the face. Do not judge under light from above."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "pressure-advance/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"**Look at the corner edge-on, against a light background:** hold the tower "
"so the tip edge is a silhouette against a window or a sheet of white paper, "
"or photograph it like that. Blobs show as a bumpy outline; the band where "
"the outline turns straight is your area."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"Where they are, turning the tower: on the **front-right face**, a 15 mm "
"straight into the **right 90° tip**; on the **back face**, the rear **seam "
"bump**, then a short straight into the **back-left 90° tip**; on the "
"**front-left face**, an 18 mm straight into the **45° tip**."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"For each band and each probe, write **✓** (pass), **L** (fails because PA "
"is too low), **H** (fails because PA is too high) or **–** (can't judge). "
"The next steps show what each looks like."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"One rule for every probe: **too low** piles plastic up **at and just "
"before** the corner (bulge, blob) and leaves the line thin after it; **too "
"high** **starves** the corner (rounded, dull, \"eaten\", small gap) and "
"leaves the line slightly fat after it."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"Ignore the floor and the first layer. Judge each band **in its middle**, "
"away from its divider groove."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "pressure-advance/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"**\"The inside corners are filled in every band, so PA must go higher.\"** "
"Do not judge PA at an inside corner: on the outside of a print a concave "
"corner is always slightly rounded, by about half a line width, whatever the "
"PA. On the first real print they looked the same in all nine bands while a "
"tip had already found the value. That column of the scorecard is not "
"counted."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "pressure-advance/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"**Every band looks identical:** PA effects are small at low speed. Reprint "
"faster, with `--flow 12` (about 147 mm/s) or your real external-perimeter "
"speed ([custom range](page:pressure-advance#download))."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "pressure-advance/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"**Bulge and gap in the same band, at the same place,** is not a PA problem. "
"Check the extruder first: a loose idler, backlash or slipping filament."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "pressure-advance/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"**The wrong temperature or wet filament mimic PA errors.** Too hot plastic "
"swells every corner; wet filament gives rough, uneven lines and blobs in "
"every band. Print at the temperature chosen with the tower, with dry "
"filament, before trusting this test."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "pressure-advance/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"**Ringing (ghosting) is not PA:** waves that repeat after each corner and "
"fade along the face come from vibrations, and are fixed with input shaping."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "pressure-advance/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"**The band boundary is not a defect:** the divider groove, and a faint line "
"where the value changes, are there on purpose."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "pressure-advance/choose"
msgid "Choose the value"
msgstr ""

#. step intro
msgctxt "pressure-advance/choose"
msgid "Apply the rule in this order; the on-screen card does it for you."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/choose"
msgid ""
"Go up from band 1 to the **lowest band where both 90° tips stop bulging**."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/choose"
msgid ""
"Take it unless it shows too-high signs (rounded tips, gaps). **Tie** with "
"the band above: take the **higher**."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/choose"
msgid ""
"Best band is band 1 or the top band? The value may lie outside the range: "
"print the other file (coarse after fine, fine after coarse)."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/choose"
msgid ""
"Go up the tower from band 1. Find the **lowest band where both 90° tips "
"have stopped bulging** (the row of blobs on the edge is gone). Ignore the "
"inside corners: they are rounded in every band."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/choose"
msgid ""
"In that band and the one above, look for **too-high signs**: tips rounded, "
"dull or \"eaten\", a gap on the 45° tip, a thin end before a tip, a gap at "
"the seam."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/choose"
msgid ""
"**Your value is the lowest band with no bulge at the 90° tips and no gap "
"anywhere.** The 45° tip may still swell very slightly there: that is normal."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/choose"
msgid ""
"**Tie:** if two neighbouring bands look equally good, take the **higher** "
"one. When in doubt, lean higher."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/choose"
msgid ""
"If the best band is **band 1 or the top band**, the right value may lie "
"outside the range: print the **coarse** file, which reaches much higher."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/choose"
msgid ""
"Example: Klipper direct drive, PETG, fine file. Bands 1 to 4 (PA 0 to "
"0.030) show a row of small blobs on the right 90° tip. Band 5 (0.040) has a "
"straight, sharp edge; the 45° tip swells a little. Band 6 (0.050) looks "
"just as good. Band 7 shows a thin end before the 45° tip. Tie between 5 and "
"6: take the higher, **0.050**."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/choose"
msgid ""
"**After a coarse tower:** print the **fine** file for your firmware and "
"extruder and score it the same way. If the coarse result lies above the "
"fine range, keep the coarse value."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/choose"
msgid ""
"Write down, next to the value: firmware, nozzle, filament, temperature, "
"test speed (98 mm/s) and acceleration."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "pressure-advance/choose"
msgid ""
"Choosing the band whose 45° tip is perfectly sharp. That is usually one "
"band too high for your 90° corners, which are the corners of your real "
"parts."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "pressure-advance/choose"
msgid ""
"If L and H marks do not sit neatly with L at the bottom and H at the top, "
"something other than PA varies along the tower. Check the Speed view in the "
"Preview again: every band must show the same speed."
msgstr ""

#. part heading (groups steps)
msgctxt "pressure-advance"
msgid "Apply the result"
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "pressure-advance/apply"
msgid "Put it in your setup"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/apply"
msgid ""
"Add the value to your **filament** start G-code (**Filament Settings › "
"Custom G-code › Start G-code**) and save it as your own preset."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/apply"
msgid ""
"The command: Klipper `SET_PRESSURE_ADVANCE ADVANCE=…`, Prusa Buddy `M572 "
"S…`, Marlin `M900 K…`, RepRapFirmware `M572 D0 S…`."
msgstr ""

#. point (warn)
msgctxt "pressure-advance/apply"
msgid ""
"**Klipper IDEX: name the extruder.** A filament start G-code without "
"`EXTRUDER=` applies to whichever tool is active when the line runs, which "
"is not always the tool that will use this filament. Write "
"`SET_PRESSURE_ADVANCE EXTRUDER=extruder{if filament_extruder_id > "
"0}{filament_extruder_id}{endif} ADVANCE=0.050`, or keep one filament preset "
"per tool. RatOS IDEX routes a plain command to the active toolhead."
msgstr ""

#. photo alt
msgctxt "pressure-advance/apply"
msgid ""
"PrusaSlicer filament settings, Custom G-code page, with "
"SET_PRESSURE_ADVANCE EXTRUDER=extruder ADVANCE=0.04 in the start G-code"
msgstr ""

#. photo caption
msgctxt "pressure-advance/apply"
msgid ""
"Filament Settings › Custom G-code › Start G-code: one line sets PA for this "
"filament, here SET_PRESSURE_ADVANCE EXTRUDER=extruder ADVANCE=0.04 "
"(Klipper)."
msgstr ""

#. More: intro
msgctxt "pressure-advance/apply"
msgid ""
"Where PA lives depends on the firmware. Setting it in the **filament** "
"profile is usually best: the value follows the spool."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/apply"
msgid ""
"**Klipper, for one value on the printer:** in `printer.cfg`, under "
"`[extruder]`, set `pressure_advance: 0.050` (on IDEX, `[extruder1]` for the "
"second toolhead), then `FIRMWARE_RESTART`."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/apply"
msgid ""
"**Klipper, per filament (recommended):** in PrusaSlicer, **Filament "
"Settings › Custom G-code › Start G-code**, add `SET_PRESSURE_ADVANCE "
"ADVANCE=0.050`. Save it as your own filament preset."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/apply"
msgid ""
"**Prusa Buddy** (MK4/MK4S, MK3.9/3.5, Core One, XL, MINI): Prusa's own "
"filament presets already contain a line such as `M572 S{if "
"nozzle_diameter[0]==0.4}0.03{elsif …}`. In **Filament Settings › Custom "
"G-code › Start G-code**, change the number for your nozzle, then save the "
"preset as your own. Old `M900 K` values are converted by the firmware, but "
"use `M572`."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/apply"
msgid ""
"**Marlin 2:** per filament, add `M900 K0.08` to the filament start G-code. "
"Or once on the printer: send `M900 K0.08`, then `M500` to store it."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/apply"
msgid ""
"**RepRapFirmware:** add `M572 D0 S0.05` to `config.g` (one line per "
"extruder drive: `D1` for the second), or put it in the filament start "
"G-code."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/apply"
msgid ""
"**PrusaSlicer 3.0** (upcoming) has native filament settings for PA "
"(`pressure_advance` and `pressure_advance_value`), and it writes the right "
"command for your G-code flavour. When you switch to it, remove any "
"hand-written PA line from the filament start G-code, so that the value is "
"not set twice. PrusaSlicer 2.9 does not have these settings."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "pressure-advance/apply"
msgid ""
"The numbers above are examples. Use the value of the band you chose, "
"written exactly as on the scorecard."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "pressure-advance/idex-apply"
msgid "IDEX and multi-tool printers"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/idex-apply"
msgid ""
"**One tower per toolhead.** Each hotend and extruder has its own PA, even "
"when two toolheads are identical: print one tower with each tool, never in "
"copy or mirror mode."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/idex-apply"
msgid ""
"Store the result **per tool**: `[extruder]` and `[extruder1]` in Klipper, "
"or one filament preset per tool in PrusaSlicer, or the `EXTRUDER=` line "
"above."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/idex-apply"
msgid ""
"**Klipper IDEX:** use `pressure-advance_klipper-dd_fine_0-0.08_T0.3mf` (or "
"`…_coarse_0-0.20_T0.3mf`) for the first toolhead and the `…_T1.3mf` twin "
"for the second. Each file is already assigned to its extruder, and its "
"commands name it (`EXTRUDER=extruder` / `EXTRUDER=extruder1`)."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/idex-apply"
msgid ""
"**Prusa XL and Marlin:** use the normal file and assign the object to the "
"tool you are testing (right-click the object › **Change extruder**). The "
"command applies to the active tool, which is the one printing."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/idex-apply"
msgid ""
"**RepRapFirmware:** the `rrf` files set `M572 D0`, extruder 0. Per-tool "
"files for other extruders are not published yet."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "pressure-advance/idex-apply"
msgid ""
"Two identical toolheads with the same filament often agree within one band. "
"If they differ by more, look at the extruders: a worn gear or a different "
"PTFE length shows up here first."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "pressure-advance/when-again"
msgid "When to run it again"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/when-again"
msgid ""
"After a new filament, nozzle, hotend, extruder or bowden tube, a large "
"temperature change, or new input shaper, speed or acceleration settings."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/when-again"
msgid "A new filament type or brand, sometimes a new colour."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/when-again"
msgid ""
"A new nozzle (size or material), hotend or extruder, or a different bowden "
"tube length."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/when-again"
msgid "A large change in printing temperature."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/when-again"
msgid ""
"A change of input shaper settings, or a big change of external-perimeter "
"speed or acceleration."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/when-again"
msgid ""
"Then, in the sequence: **flow**, then **retraction**. Both read better once "
"PA is right."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "pressure-advance/share"
msgid "Share your result"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/share"
msgid ""
"Printed the test? **Post your Make** on the [Printables "
"page](link:printables-pressure-advance) with a photo of your **best and "
"your worst** band, and say which value you chose."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/share"
msgid ""
"Makes help other users judge their own print, and they help the project "
"grow."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/share"
msgid ""
"We regularly pick the clearest Makes and ask their authors whether we may "
"use the photo in this official guide, with their name and a link."
msgstr ""

#. part heading (groups steps)
msgctxt "pressure-advance"
msgid "Troubleshooting"
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "pressure-advance/troubleshooting"
msgid "Every band looks the same"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**No markers on the slider:** the file was imported instead of opened, came "
"from a ZIP, was dropped together with other files, or PrusaSlicer is older "
"than 2.7. Open the single .3mf with **File › Open project**."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**The walls printed slowly:** check the Speed view ([the test "
"speed](page:pressure-advance#speed)). If it shows less than 98 mm/s, the "
"file was imported instead of opened (no `M220` markers), or your filament's "
"**Slow down if layer print time is below** is above 28 s: lower it for this "
"print only."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**The next print runs far too fast:** the PA print was cancelled before its "
"top, and the speed factor is still set. Send `M220 S100` (Klipper: or "
"`RESTART`)."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**Markers are there but nothing changed:** the printer ignored the command. "
"Check that the file matches your firmware family. **Marlin:** linear "
"advance is an option of the firmware build; without `LIN_ADVANCE`, `M900` "
"does nothing. Send `M900` alone from a terminal: if it does not answer with "
"a K value, your firmware has no linear advance."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**Something resets PA during the print:** a **Before / After layer change "
"G-code**, or a macro, that sets PA. Remove it for this print."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**The range is too narrow to see a change:** all bands look good or all "
"look bad. Print the **coarse** file."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**The test speed is too low for your printer:** PA effects are small at low "
"speed. If you normally print outer walls faster than 98 mm/s, reprint with "
"`--flow 12` (about 147 mm/s) or `--speed <your external-perimeter speed>` "
"([custom range](page:pressure-advance#download))."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**You were looking at the inside corners:** they look the same in every "
"band whatever the PA. Read the tips instead ([how to "
"read](page:pressure-advance#how-to-read))."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "pressure-advance/troubleshooting-more"
msgid "Other problems"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**The printer stops with an error at a band change** (for example `Unknown "
"command` or a Klipper error about an extruder): the file is for another "
"firmware family, or, on IDEX, for another tool. Use the file for your "
"firmware and for the tool the object is assigned to."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**Even the top band bulges:** the range is too low (print the coarse file), "
"or the temperature is too high. Check the temperature first."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**Even band 2 shows gaps:** the range is too high (print the fine file), or "
"the extruder under-extrudes."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**Bulge and gap at the same place, in the same band:** not PA. Check the "
"extruder: idler tension, gear backlash, filament slipping or a partly "
"clogged nozzle."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**The 45° tip broke off or curled up:** score it –; the 90° tips decide."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**Lines in every band look rough or bubbly, or hiss when printing:** wet "
"filament. Dry it and print again."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "pressure-advance/troubleshooting-more"
msgid "**PrusaSlicer 3.x alpha:** not tested with these files yet."
msgstr ""

#. page title
msgctxt "temperature-tower-scorecard"
msgid "Temperature tower scorecard"
msgstr ""

#. page description
msgctxt "temperature-tower-scorecard"
msgid ""
"A printable scoring card for the temperature tower: pass, too hot or too "
"cold for each feature of each block."
msgstr ""

#. page intro
msgctxt "temperature-tower-scorecard"
msgid ""
"Score each block, then let the rules pick the temperature: click the cells "
"on screen, or print the card and use a pen. How to judge each feature: "
"[temperature tower guide](page:temperature-tower#how-to-read).\n"
"\n"
"Write down too: filament **brand, type, colour**, the **file** (coarse 10 "
"°C or fine 5 °C), **nozzle**, **test flow (10 mm³/s)**, **date**. A card "
"without them is useless next month.\n"
msgstr ""

#. page breadcrumb
msgctxt "temperature-tower-scorecard"
msgid "Printable card"
msgstr ""

#. callout
msgctxt "temperature-tower-scorecard"
msgid ""
"The columns follow the hexagonal tower model v2.3.0, in reading order: "
"**Strings first** (before you touch the tower), the **Flag** last. Label "
"and Surface only confirm; the **Hole** column is not counted at all. Pick "
"your file in the menu (coarse files have 10 °C steps, fine files 5 °C). "
"**TPU:** mark the Flag column – on every block. Printed on A4: the rules on "
"page 1, the whole grid on page 2."
msgstr ""

#. page title
msgctxt "temperature-tower"
msgid "Temperature tower"
msgstr ""

#. page description
msgctxt "temperature-tower"
msgid ""
"Find the nozzle temperature for a filament with a compact tower printed hot "
"to cold, scored block by block."
msgstr ""

#. page intro
msgctxt "temperature-tower"
msgid ""
"A hexagonal tower printed hot to cold, one step colder per 7 mm block. "
"Score each block, let a few fixed rules pick the winner, and type that "
"temperature into your filament profile.\n"
"\n"
"About 40 minutes and 7 to 10 g of filament, with **no changes to your "
"profiles**.\n"
msgstr ""

#. More: intro
msgctxt "temperature-tower"
msgid ""
"Each block is printed one step colder than the one below: **10 °C** on the "
"**coarse** file, **5 °C** on the **fine** one. Every block carries the same "
"features, one group per face: four that decide (the snap flag, the string "
"ladder, a steep overhang ramp and a 22 mm bridge), two that only confirm "
"(the engraved temperature and a smooth face), and a round hole you look "
"through that is not counted. Each fails in a recognisable way when the "
"plastic is too hot or too cold.\n"
"\n"
"The tower prints at a fixed flow of 10 mm³/s (TPU at 3 mm³/s, so it takes "
"much longer). The time was measured on the author's printer, the grams are "
"our estimate; PrusaSlicer shows the figures for your profiles after "
"slicing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. callout
msgctxt "temperature-tower"
msgid ""
"Open the file with **File › Open project**. Never with **Import**, never as "
"a ZIP: an import keeps the shape but silently drops the temperature "
"changes, and you would print a whole tower at one temperature."
msgstr ""

#. box title
msgctxt "temperature-tower"
msgid "What you need"
msgstr ""

#. box column title
msgctxt "temperature-tower"
msgid "To print"
msgstr ""

#. box item
msgctxt "temperature-tower"
msgid "The [tower file for your material](page:temperature-tower#download)"
msgstr ""

#. box item
msgctxt "temperature-tower"
msgid "PrusaSlicer 2.9 (2.7 is the minimum that opens the project)"
msgstr ""

#. box item
msgctxt "temperature-tower"
msgid "Your own printer, print and filament profiles, already working"
msgstr ""

#. box item
msgctxt "temperature-tower"
msgid "The filament to test, **dry**"
msgstr ""

#. box item
msgctxt "temperature-tower"
msgid "7 to 10 g of it"
msgstr ""

#. box column title
msgctxt "temperature-tower"
msgid "To read it"
msgstr ""

#. box item
msgctxt "temperature-tower"
msgid "A lamp you can move, and a dark card or cloth"
msgstr ""

#. box item
msgctxt "temperature-tower"
msgid ""
"The [scorecard](page:temperature-tower-scorecard), printed or on screen"
msgstr ""

#. box item
msgctxt "temperature-tower"
msgid "A lamp or a bright window to look through the string ladder"
msgstr ""

#. box item
msgctxt "temperature-tower"
msgid "Optional: a phone camera, to zoom on fine text"
msgstr ""

#. part heading (groups steps)
msgctxt "temperature-tower"
msgid "Before printing"
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "temperature-tower/what-for"
msgid "What it is for, and when to run it"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/what-for"
msgid ""
"It finds the **nozzle temperature** that gives strong layers without "
"strings, sagging or melted details, **for your printer, your speed and this "
"filament**."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/what-for"
msgid ""
"Run it for every new filament **type** or **brand**, after the first layer "
"and before pressure advance."
msgstr ""

#. photo alt
msgctxt "temperature-tower/what-for"
msgid "The whole temperature tower, front view, PETG"
msgstr ""

#. photo caption
msgctxt "temperature-tower/what-for"
msgid ""
"The tower: the hottest block at the bottom, one step colder at each block "
"going up."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/what-for"
msgid ""
"Run it for every new filament **type** or **brand**, and when a colour "
"behaves differently from the last spool."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/what-for"
msgid ""
"Run it again after changing the hotend, the nozzle size or material (a "
"hardened steel nozzle usually wants it a little hotter), or when you start "
"printing much faster."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/what-for"
msgid ""
"In the full sequence it comes **after the first layer** is right and "
"**before** pressure advance, flow and retraction: those all depend on the "
"temperature you choose here."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "temperature-tower/what-for"
msgid ""
"The temperature printed on the spool is a range, often 40 °C wide. This "
"test tells you where in that range your printer is happiest."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "temperature-tower/what-for"
msgid ""
"The result is valid for the flow and cooling the tower prints with: **10 "
"mm³/s** and your filament's big-part fan setting. If you print at a very "
"different volumetric flow, the best temperature moves (usually hotter for "
"faster)."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "temperature-tower/coarse-or-fine"
msgid "Coarse or fine: which file first"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/coarse-or-fine"
msgid "**Known brand and type:** print the **fine** file (5 °C steps)."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/coarse-or-fine"
msgid ""
"**New or unknown filament:** print the **coarse** file (10 °C steps), then "
"a fine pass around its best block."
msgstr ""

#. More: intro
msgctxt "temperature-tower/coarse-or-fine"
msgid ""
"Every material has two files. Print the coarse one **only if you do not "
"know the filament's range well**."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/coarse-or-fine"
msgid ""
"**Fine (5 °C steps):** the usual range for the material. Start here with a "
"known brand and type, for example a PLA or PETG that prints like the ones "
"you already use."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/coarse-or-fine"
msgid ""
"**Coarse (10 °C steps):** a wider range, for a new brand, an unusual blend, "
"a spool without a label, or any filament whose printing range you are not "
"sure of. It tells you roughly where the filament is happy."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/coarse-or-fine"
msgid ""
"**Then a fine pass around the best coarse block:** print the **fine** file "
"for your material (5 °C steps, table below). If the best coarse block lies "
"outside the fine file's range, take that block's temperature: the coarse "
"result is your value."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/coarse-or-fine"
msgid ""
"If the coarse tower's best block is at its top or bottom, the right "
"temperature may be outside the range: check the manufacturer's range before "
"going further."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "temperature-tower/coarse-or-fine"
msgid ""
"`--centre` makes 5 blocks (centre ± 10 °C); add `--half-blocks 3` for 7. "
"Custom files are named like `temp-tower_PETG-custom_250-230_5C.3mf`."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid "Download the right file"
msgstr ""

#. point (warn)
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid ""
"**Above about 250 °C, hotends with a PTFE (Teflon) tube down to the nozzle "
"are not safe:** the tube degrades and gives off harmful fumes. The PETG, "
"ABS/ASA and PC files all go above 250 °C: print them only on an all-metal "
"hotend, and check your hotend's maximum temperature first."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid "Get it from the {suite} page on Printables (below)."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid ""
"Download the **single .3mf file** named in the table, for example "
"`temp-tower_PETG_fine_260-220_5C.3mf`: the name must end in **.3mf**. Never "
"\"Download all\" as a ZIP."
msgstr ""

#. table header
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid "Material"
msgstr ""

#. table header
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid "Coarse: bottom → top"
msgstr ""

#. table header
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid "Fine: bottom → top"
msgstr ""

#. table header
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid "Filament (coarse / fine)"
msgstr ""

#. table header
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid "Notes"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid "PLA"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid ""
"`temp-tower_PLA_coarse_240-180_10C.3mf`\n"
"240 → 180 °C, 7 blocks"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid ""
"`temp-tower_PLA_fine_230-190_5C.3mf`\n"
"230 → 190 °C, 9 blocks"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid "8.0 / 10.2 g"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid "The reference material; start with the fine file if unsure."
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid "PETG"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid ""
"`temp-tower_PETG_coarse_270-200_10C.3mf`\n"
"270 → 200 °C, 8 blocks"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid ""
"`temp-tower_PETG_fine_260-220_5C.3mf`\n"
"260 → 220 °C, 9 blocks"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid "9.3 / 10.4 g"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid "Both files go above 250 °C: all-metal hotend only."
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid "ABS / ASA"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid ""
"`temp-tower_ABS-ASA_coarse_280-220_10C.3mf`\n"
"280 → 220 °C, 7 blocks"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid ""
"`temp-tower_ABS-ASA_fine_270-230_5C.3mf`\n"
"270 → 230 °C, 9 blocks"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid "6.9 / 8.7 g"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid ""
"All-metal hotend. Enclosure closed and warm before you start. The files add "
"a 4 mm brim (included in the grams)."
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid "TPU"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid ""
"`temp-tower_TPU_coarse_250-200_10C.3mf`\n"
"250 → 200 °C, 6 blocks"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid ""
"`temp-tower_TPU_fine_240-210_5C.3mf`\n"
"240 → 210 °C, 7 blocks"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid "6.8 / 7.8 g"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid ""
"Prints at 3 mm³/s; direct drive recommended. The snap flag is not scored."
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid "PC"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid ""
"`temp-tower_PC_coarse_310-250_10C.3mf`\n"
"310 → 250 °C, 7 blocks"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid ""
"`temp-tower_PC_fine_300-260_5C.3mf`\n"
"300 → 260 °C, 9 blocks"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid "7.9 / 10.0 g"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid ""
"Prints at 8 mm³/s. All-metal hotend rated for 310 °C, enclosure, hot bed. "
"The files add a 4 mm brim (included in the grams)."
msgstr ""

#. More: intro
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid ""
"Two files per material family, coarse and fine. Each starts hot at the "
"bottom and goes down one step per block."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid ""
"Every block is 7 mm tall, on a 0.6 mm floor, with a 0.6 mm cap on top: 7 "
"blocks make a 50.2 mm tower, 8 blocks 57.2 mm, 9 blocks 64.2 mm."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid ""
"Print time, measured on the author's printer (PETG): about 38 minutes for "
"the coarse tower and 40 for the fine one. TPU prints at 3 mm³/s and takes "
"much longer. The file prints with 0.45 mm lines and 0.2 mm layers; the "
"grams assume 1.75 mm filament and no skirt. After slicing, PrusaSlicer "
"shows the figures for your profiles."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid ""
"The files are named like `temp-tower_PETG_coarse_270-200_10C.3mf` and "
"`temp-tower_PETG_fine_260-220_5C.3mf`: material, variant, the bottom and "
"top temperatures, and the **step**. They are made for a **0.4 mm nozzle**."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid ""
"Download the **single .3mf file**, not \"Download all\" as a ZIP: "
"PrusaSlicer opens a ZIP's 3MF as plain geometry and the temperature changes "
"are lost."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid ""
"**IDEX and multi-tool printers:** print the whole tower with **one tool**: "
"in PrusaSlicer, set the object's extruder to that tool. The temperature "
"commands (`M104` without a tool number) act on the active tool. "
"`temp-tower_PETG_coarse_270-200_10C_T0.3mf` is a ready example already set "
"to T0; for another tool, use the plain file and set the extruder."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/download"
msgid ""
"**Bambu Studio and OrcaSlicer:** the files are made for PrusaSlicer. Other "
"slicers may load the shape only, without the temperature changes."
msgstr ""

#. part heading (groups steps)
msgctxt "temperature-tower"
msgid "Print it"
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "temperature-tower/open-project"
msgid "Open it as a project"
msgstr ""

#. callout title
msgctxt "temperature-tower/open-project"
msgid "Before you print: one marker per block"
msgstr ""

#. callout
msgctxt "temperature-tower/open-project"
msgid ""
"After opening, slice and look at the vertical layer slider in the Preview: "
"it must show **one marker per block**. Hovering a marker shows `M104 "
"S<temp>`."
msgstr ""

#. callout item
msgctxt "temperature-tower/open-project"
msgid ""
"On multi-extruder printer profiles (IDEX, XL, MMU) there may be more than "
"one marker per block. That is fine."
msgstr ""

#. callout item
msgctxt "temperature-tower/open-project"
msgid ""
"**No markers: do not print.** Open the file again with **File › Open "
"project**, and check in **Preferences** that the load project dialog is on "
"(see below)."
msgstr ""

#. callout item
msgctxt "temperature-tower/open-project"
msgid ""
"Quick check: export the G-code and search it for \"M104\": [details in the "
"next step](page:temperature-tower#check)."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/open-project"
msgid ""
"In PrusaSlicer, **File › Open project** (Ctrl+O, or ⌘O on macOS) and choose "
"the .3mf."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/open-project"
msgid ""
"Dragging the file in? Choose **Open as project**, never **Import 3D models "
"only**."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/open-project"
msgid "Do not scale, rotate, cut or sink the tower."
msgstr ""

#. point (warn)
msgctxt "temperature-tower/open-project"
msgid ""
"Opening the project replaces anything already on the plate, and any custom "
"G-code you had added there. The temperature changes belong to the plate, so "
"print the tower **on its own**."
msgstr ""

#. photo alt
msgctxt "temperature-tower/open-project"
msgid "PrusaSlicer's drop dialog with Open as project highlighted"
msgstr ""

#. photo caption
msgctxt "temperature-tower/open-project"
msgid "Dragging the file in: choose Open as project."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/open-project"
msgid ""
"If you drag the file onto the window instead, PrusaSlicer asks what to do: "
"choose **Open as project**. **Import 3D models only** drops the temperature "
"changes."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/open-project"
msgid ""
"If you once ticked \"don't show again\" with the wrong choice, dragging "
"will import silently. Turn the question back on in **Preferences › General "
"› Show load project dialog**."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/open-project"
msgid ""
"Downloads started from Printables inside PrusaSlicer go through the same "
"question: answer **Open as project**."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/open-project"
msgid ""
"The project carries **no printer, filament or print profile**: PrusaSlicer "
"keeps yours. It only carries the tower, a few per-object settings the test "
"needs (0.2 mm layers, 0.45 mm lines, two perimeters, Arachne walls, 15 % "
"infill, no supports, the seam at the back, the test flow) and the "
"temperature changes."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "temperature-tower/open-project"
msgid ""
"Do not scale, rotate, cut or sink the tower. The temperature changes are "
"fixed heights: if the tower changes size, the changes land in the wrong "
"place."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "temperature-tower/check"
msgid "Check that the temperature changes are there"
msgstr ""

#. callout title
msgctxt "temperature-tower/check"
msgid "No markers, no print"
msgstr ""

#. callout
msgctxt "temperature-tower/check"
msgid ""
"The vertical slider must show **one marker per block**, at the first layer "
"of each block. Hovering a marker shows `M104 S<temp>`."
msgstr ""

#. callout item
msgctxt "temperature-tower/check"
msgid ""
"On multi-extruder printer profiles (IDEX, XL, MMU) there may be more than "
"one marker per block. That is fine: they all carry the same temperature."
msgstr ""

#. callout item
msgctxt "temperature-tower/check"
msgid ""
"**If no markers are visible, do not print.** The temperature changes were "
"dropped and the whole tower would print at one temperature. Open the file "
"again with **File › Open project** (not Import), and check **Preferences › "
"General › Show load project dialog** so that dragging a file asks you again."
msgstr ""

#. callout item
msgctxt "temperature-tower/check"
msgid ""
"Quick check before printing: export the G-code and search it for \"M104\". "
"Every block's temperature must be there, going down by the file's step (10 "
"°C coarse, 5 °C fine)."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/check"
msgid ""
"Pick **your** printer, print and filament profiles, click **Slice now** and "
"switch to **Preview**."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/check"
msgid ""
"Hover the markers on the layer slider: the `M104` values must go down one "
"step per block."
msgstr ""

#. photo alt
msgctxt "temperature-tower/check"
msgid ""
"PrusaSlicer preview of the temperature tower: one custom G-code marker per "
"block on the layer slider, the block 5 tooltip showing M104 S230 and M220 "
"S1445"
msgstr ""

#. photo caption
msgctxt "temperature-tower/check"
msgid "One marker per block; hover to read the temperature."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/check"
msgid ""
"Pick **your** printer, print and filament profiles in the right-hand panel, "
"as for any print."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/check"
msgid ""
"Click **Slice now** and switch to **Preview**. On the vertical layer slider "
"there is a small marker at the first layer of each block."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/check"
msgid ""
"Hover a marker: it shows the custom G-code, for example `M104 S240` and "
"`M220 S1445` (the test flow, [see below](page:temperature-tower#flow)). The "
"values must go down by the file's step going up the tower: 10 °C on a "
"coarse file, 5 °C on a fine one. The last marker, at the top, sets `M220 "
"S100` back."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/check"
msgid ""
"No markers on the slider? The file was imported, not opened: [open it as a "
"project](page:temperature-tower#open-project)."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "temperature-tower/check"
msgid ""
"In the exported G-code each block starts with a comment such as `;CALIB "
"temp_tower block=3/8 temp=250` followed by `M104 S250` and `M220 S1445`. "
"The same line may appear more than once in a block (see "
"[why](page:temperature-tower#troubleshooting)); that is harmless. `M104` "
"without a tool number acts on the active tool, which is why the whole tower "
"must be printed with one tool."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "temperature-tower/flow"
msgid "The test flow: nothing to change"
msgstr ""

#. callout title
msgctxt "temperature-tower/flow"
msgid "If you cancel the print, send M220 S100"
msgstr ""

#. callout
msgctxt "temperature-tower/flow"
msgid ""
"The file speeds the printer up with `M220`, and the cap at the top sets it "
"back with `M220 S100`. If you **cancel** the print before the top, type "
"**`M220 S100`** in the printer's console (or terminal): Klipper, Marlin and "
"RepRapFirmware keep the factor, and your next print would run many times "
"too fast. Prusa Buddy printers (MK4, Core One, XL, MINI) reset it at the "
"next print start by themselves."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/flow"
msgid ""
"**No profile changes are needed:** the blocks print at a fixed **10 mm³/s** "
"(TPU 3, PC 8)."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/flow"
msgid ""
"To check, switch the Preview legend to **Volumetric flow rate**: about **10 "
"mm³/s** in every block."
msgstr ""

#. photo alt
msgctxt "temperature-tower/flow"
msgid ""
"PrusaSlicer preview of the temperature tower in Volumetric flow rate view, "
"the blocks at about 10 mm³/s"
msgstr ""

#. photo caption
msgctxt "temperature-tower/flow"
msgid ""
"Preview, Volumetric flow rate: every block at about 10 mm³/s, with your "
"profiles unchanged."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/flow"
msgid ""
"**No profile changes are needed.** The blocks print at a fixed flow of **10 "
"mm³/s** (123 mm/s with the file's 0.2 mm layers and 0.45 mm lines): a "
"moderate, typical flow. TPU prints at 3 mm³/s, PC at 8."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/flow"
msgid ""
"How: the file asks PrusaSlicer for a very low speed (about 8.5 mm/s), so "
"the slicer sees long layers and never slows them down for cooling. Every "
"block also sends a speed factor of about **`M220 S1450`** that the printer "
"applies (8.5 × 14.45 ≈ 123 mm/s; the exact value is in each file). `M220` "
"works on every firmware, so the files stay universal."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/flow"
msgid ""
"The floor, the first layer and the 0.6 mm cap at the top print at **100 "
"%**, with your own speeds. Bridges and overhangs print at 25 mm/s."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/flow"
msgid ""
"**Check in the Preview:** slice, and in the legend at the bottom left "
"switch the view to **Volumetric flow rate**: the walls show about **10 "
"mm³/s** in every block (**Speed**: about 123 mm/s). The print time is the "
"real one. This works with Klipper, Marlin and Prusa printer profiles."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/flow"
msgid ""
"**RepRapFirmware printer profiles:** PrusaSlicer ignores `M220` in the "
"preview there. The legend shows the low base speed and the time is many "
"times too long; the printer still prints at full speed."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/flow"
msgid ""
"**Fan: big-part cooling.** The slicer sees long layers, so the fan runs at "
"your filament's **minimum** fan speed, with the **bridge fan** on the "
"bridge, the ladder rungs and the overhang tongues: exactly how a big part "
"is cooled. The real layers take only a few seconds, so the ladder posts and "
"the flag get a little less time to cool than on a big part: their hot-side "
"failures come slightly early, which points you to a slightly cooler, safer "
"temperature."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "temperature-tower/flow"
msgid ""
"**Another flow:** generate a file with `--flow <mm³/s>` or `--speed <mm/s>` "
"(`python3 -I calib.py temp-tower --material PETG --flow 12`, in the {suite} "
"[repository](link:repository)). PrusaSlicer can no longer cap the flow with "
"your filament's **max volumetric speed**, so stay below it: use about **0.8 "
"× your filament's max volumetric speed** (with 15 mm³/s, `--flow 12`), or "
"the flow you usually print at."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "temperature-tower/settings"
msgid "Set the filament profile"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/settings"
msgid ""
"In **Filament Settings › Filament › Temperature**, set **First layer** and "
"**Other layers** both to the **bottom block's** temperature."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/settings"
msgid "Leave everything else as in your normal profile."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/settings"
msgid ""
"In **Filament Settings › Filament › Temperature**, set **Nozzle: First "
"layer** and **Other layers** both to the **bottom block's** temperature "
"(240 °C for the PLA coarse tower, 230 °C for the fine one). The tower "
"starts with a 0.6 mm solid floor, printed at that temperature too."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/settings"
msgid ""
"Why both: PrusaSlicer switches to the \"Other layers\" temperature at the "
"second layer. If that value is different, it overrides the first block and "
"your bottom block is printed at the wrong temperature."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/settings"
msgid ""
"Leave the bed temperature, fan and everything else as in your normal "
"profile: the file sets the flow, your profile does the rest."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "temperature-tower/settings"
msgid ""
"Skirt loops at 0 save filament (see [Before you "
"start](page:before-you-start#global-settings)). A brim is fine if the tower "
"tends to come loose."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "temperature-tower/print"
msgid "Slice and print"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/print"
msgid ""
"Slice again after any change, export the G-code (or send it to the printer) "
"and start the print."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/print"
msgid "Watch the first block go down: it must stick well."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/print"
msgid "Let the tower cool completely before you take it off the bed."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/print"
msgid ""
"Watch the first block go down: it must stick well. A tower that comes loose "
"halfway is wasted filament."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/print"
msgid ""
"When the nozzle reaches each new block, the target temperature on the "
"printer's screen drops by one step (10 or 5 °C). The printer does **not** "
"stop to wait: it keeps printing while the hotend cools, so the first 1–2 "
"layers of each block are in between."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/print"
msgid ""
"Let the tower cool completely before taking it off the bed, and before the "
"snap test."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "temperature-tower/print"
msgid ""
"Write the filament brand, colour, nozzle and print speed on a piece of tape "
"on the tower. In a month you will not remember which tower was which."
msgstr ""

#. part heading (groups steps)
msgctxt "temperature-tower"
msgid "Read it"
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "temperature-tower/how-to-read"
msgid "How to read it: score each block"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"Open the [scorecard](page:temperature-tower-scorecard). Hold the tower with "
"the **engraved numbers facing you**: block 1, at the bottom, is the hottest."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"Mark each feature of each block **✓**, **H** (too hot), **C** (too cold) or "
"**–**, judging above the block's bottom 1 mm."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"Score the **strings first**, before you touch the tower, and break the "
"**snap flags last**."
msgstr ""

#. photo alt
msgctxt "temperature-tower/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"Top view of one block of the hexagonal tower, the front face at the bottom"
msgstr ""

#. photo caption
msgctxt "temperature-tower/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"One block seen from above, front (numbers) at the bottom. Anticlockwise "
"from the front: the smooth face, the ramp tongues, the plain back face, the "
"string ladder on the back corner, the flag at the edge of its shelf, and "
"the bridge beam on its two long ribs; the front rib (bottom left) carries "
"the hole."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"Open the [scorecard](page:temperature-tower-scorecard) (print it, or fill "
"it on screen). One row per block, one column per feature."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"Hold the tower with the **engraved numbers facing you**, then turn it "
"clockwise (seen from above), one face at a time: **front**, the "
"temperature; **front-right**, the plain smooth face; **back-right**, four "
"overhang tongues; **back**, plain (the seam is there); on the **back "
"corner**, the string ladder; **back-left**, a shelf with the thin snap flag "
"at its outer edge; **front-left**, plain, with the long bridge beam in "
"front of it; the beam's **front rib**, next to the numbers, has a round "
"hole in every block."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"Block 1 is the **bottom** of the tower, the **hottest**. Each block's "
"temperature is engraved on its front."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"For each block and each feature, write **✓** (pass), **H** (fails because "
"too hot), **C** (fails because too cold) or **–** (can't judge). The next "
"steps show what each looks like."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"Judge each block **above its bottom 1 mm**: there the temperature was still "
"changing, and no feature starts there."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"Score the features in the order of the steps below: **the strings first, "
"before you touch or brush the tower** (photograph the ladder now if you "
"want a record), and **the snap flags last**: once broken, they cannot be "
"looked at again. Write the ramp angle in the Notes column."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"**Not every feature changes from block to block.** On the author's coarse "
"PETG tower (270 → 200 °C, at the file's flow) the strings showed the hot "
"end, and the smooth face and the top of the bridge beam showed the cold "
"end; the label, the hole and the ramp tongues seen from the side looked the "
"same on every block. A column that does not change gets ✓ on every block: "
"the flags and the columns that do change decide. The label and the smooth "
"face are marked *Confirms only*; the hole is marked *Confirms* and is not "
"counted at all."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"Then apply the [rules for choosing the "
"block](page:temperature-tower#choose). The on-screen card applies them for "
"you."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "temperature-tower/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"You do not need to know why a feature fails. You only need to compare "
"blocks: the descriptions tell you which way each failure points."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "temperature-tower/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"Reading the tower upside down, or counting from the top. Always find the "
"engraved temperature before you score a row."
msgstr ""

#. More: photo alt
msgctxt "temperature-tower/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"The fine PETG tower on a dark card next to a tablet showing the scorecard"
msgstr ""

#. More: photo caption
msgctxt "temperature-tower/how-to-read"
msgid ""
"A good reading set-up: dark background, light from one side, the scorecard "
"to hand."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "temperature-tower/not-scored"
msgid "Also worth a look (not scored)"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/not-scored"
msgid ""
"Not scored, but worth a glance: the layer lines, the tower's outline, "
"colour and gloss."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/not-scored"
msgid ""
"**Layer lines:** uneven or wobbly lines that look the same on every block "
"are a mechanical or extrusion problem, not temperature."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/not-scored"
msgid ""
"**The whole tower leaning or with a wavy outline:** the tower is slim; a "
"loose bed, a wobbly gantry or a weak first layer shows up here first."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/not-scored"
msgid ""
"**Colour and gloss bands:** many filaments get glossier when hotter. A "
"useful hint, already scored under the smooth face."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "temperature-tower/choose"
msgid "Choose the temperature"
msgstr ""

#. step intro
msgctxt "temperature-tower/choose"
msgid "Apply the rules in this order; the on-screen card does it for you."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/choose"
msgid "Cross out every block whose **snap flag** failed (not for TPU)."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/choose"
msgid "Of the rest, the block with the **most ✓** wins."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/choose"
msgid ""
"Tie: fewer failures (H + C) wins. Still a tie: the **cooler** block wins."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/choose"
msgid ""
"For parts that must be strong, go **one block hotter** than the block the "
"rules picked."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/choose"
msgid ""
"Example: PLA fine tower, blocks 220, 215 and 210 °C all score 5 ✓ with one "
"failure each, block 205 fails the snap flag. The cooler of the tied blocks "
"wins: **210 °C**. For strong parts, use 215 °C."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/choose"
msgid ""
"Example from the author's coarse tower, PETG 270 → 200: the strings are "
"clearly worse at 250, 260 and 270 °C (H); the top of the bridge is rough at "
"230 °C and colder, the smooth face streaky from 220 °C down (C). 240 °C is "
"the only block with neither; if its flag holds, the rules pick **240 °C**, "
"and a fine pass 250 → 230 then settles it to 5 °C."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "temperature-tower/choose"
msgid ""
"Choosing the block with the **cleanest ladder**. The coldest blocks string "
"the least and are the weakest: on the author's PETG the 200–220 °C blocks "
"had almost no strings, but a streaky face and rough bridges. That is why "
"the snap flag comes first."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "temperature-tower/choose"
msgid ""
"Taking the coldest block with no strings. Stringing is also a retraction "
"problem, tuned later in the sequence."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "temperature-tower/choose"
msgid ""
"If the H marks do not all sit below the C marks, something other than "
"temperature is varying across the tower: cooling, wet filament, or "
"transition layers read as blocks. Fix that before trusting the result."
msgstr ""

#. part heading (groups steps)
msgctxt "temperature-tower"
msgid "Apply the result"
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "temperature-tower/apply"
msgid "Put it in your filament profile"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/apply"
msgid ""
"In **Filament Settings › Filament › Temperature**, set **Nozzle › Other "
"layers** to the chosen block's temperature."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/apply"
msgid "Save it as a new profile, for example `Brand PLA White – 215`."
msgstr ""

#. photo alt
msgctxt "temperature-tower/apply"
msgid ""
"PrusaSlicer filament settings with the nozzle first layer and other layers "
"fields"
msgstr ""

#. photo caption
msgctxt "temperature-tower/apply"
msgid "Filament Settings › Filament › Temperature: the two nozzle fields."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/apply"
msgid "Open **Filament Settings › Filament › Temperature**."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/apply"
msgid ""
"Set **Nozzle › Other layers** (`temperature`) to the chosen block's "
"temperature."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/apply"
msgid ""
"Set **Nozzle › First layer** (`first_layer_temperature`): see the next step."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/apply"
msgid ""
"Save the profile under a new name with the save icon next to the preset "
"name, for example `Brand PLA White – 215`. Keep the original as it was."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/apply"
msgid ""
"The temperature lives in the **filament** profile, not in the printer or "
"print profile, so it follows the spool to any printer profile you use it "
"with."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "temperature-tower/first-layer"
msgid "First layer and other layers"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/first-layer"
msgid ""
"Set **First layer** about **5 °C hotter** than the other layers, never "
"above the hottest block that scored well."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/first-layer"
msgid ""
"The tower tells you the temperature for **all layers after the first**."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/first-layer"
msgid ""
"For the first layer, a common choice is **5 °C hotter** than the other "
"layers: the plastic flows a little better into the bed and sticks better. "
"Prusa's own PLA profiles do this."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/first-layer"
msgid ""
"Do not go above the hottest block that scored well, and keep it the same if "
"your first layers already stick fine."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/first-layer"
msgid ""
"PETG often prints its first layer up to 10 °C hotter; TPU usually needs no "
"difference."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "temperature-tower/first-layer"
msgid ""
"If you also use `idle_temperature` (only active with **Ooze prevention**), "
"set it from a standby-temperature test (planned), not from this tower."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "temperature-tower/idex-apply"
msgid "IDEX and multi-tool printers"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/idex-apply"
msgid "Give the updated profile to every extruder that uses this filament."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/idex-apply"
msgid "Different hotends or nozzles each need their own tower."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/idex-apply"
msgid ""
"The result belongs to the filament **and the hotend** you tested. Two "
"identical hotends with the same filament usually agree within a block; "
"different hotends or nozzles each need their own tower."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/idex-apply"
msgid ""
"In PrusaSlicer each extruder uses its own filament profile: make sure every "
"extruder that will use this filament gets the updated profile."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/idex-apply"
msgid ""
"Standby temperatures for the idle tool are a separate test (planned): this "
"tower only sets the printing temperature."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "temperature-tower/when-again"
msgid "When to run it again"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/when-again"
msgid ""
"A new brand or type of filament, or a colour that behaves differently."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/when-again"
msgid "A new hotend, heater, thermistor or nozzle (size or material)."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/when-again"
msgid "A big jump in print speed or volumetric flow."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/when-again"
msgid ""
"Then, in the sequence: **maximum volumetric flow**, then **pressure "
"advance**, then **flow**. Pressure advance in particular must be redone "
"whenever the temperature changes a lot."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "temperature-tower/share"
msgid "Share your result"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/share"
msgid ""
"Printed the test? **Post your Make** on the [Printables "
"page](link:printables-temperature-tower) with a photo of your **best and "
"your worst** block, and say which value you chose."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/share"
msgid ""
"Makes help other users judge their own print, and they help the project "
"grow."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/share"
msgid ""
"We regularly pick the clearest Makes and ask their authors whether we may "
"use the photo in this official guide, with their name and a link."
msgstr ""

#. part heading (groups steps)
msgctxt "temperature-tower"
msgid "Troubleshooting"
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "temperature-tower/troubleshooting"
msgid "Every block looks the same"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**No markers on the slider:** open the single .3mf with **File › Open "
"project**, not Import."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**Strings and weak flags on every block:** wet filament. Dry it and print "
"again."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**The next print runs far too fast:** send `M220 S100` (Klipper: or "
"`RESTART`)."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**No markers on the slider:** the file was imported instead of opened, came "
"from a ZIP, was dropped together with other files, or PrusaSlicer is older "
"than 2.7. With **Import** instead of **Open project**, PrusaSlicer loads "
"the shape and silently drops every temperature code: nothing warns you. "
"Open the single .3mf with **File › Open project**."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**Why each temperature code is repeated:** in the file, every block's "
"`M104` is repeated on a 0.01 mm grid over the first few tenths of a "
"millimetre of the block. On multi-extruder printer profiles (IDEX, XL, "
"MMU), PrusaSlicer keeps a custom G-code only if it lands exactly on the top "
"of a layer, and the file cannot know your layer height. The grid makes sure "
"that one copy always lands on a layer top, for any layer height in steps of "
"0.01 mm. On a single-tool profile the copies merge into one marker per "
"block; on a multi-extruder profile you may see two, with the same "
"temperature."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**Markers are there but the printer never changed temperature:** look at "
"the printer's screen while it prints. Some start or tool-change macros "
"reset the temperature; with a single tool and no tool changes this should "
"not happen."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**The bottom block was printed at the wrong temperature:** the filament "
"profile's First layer and Other layers temperatures were different ([see "
"why](page:temperature-tower#settings))."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**Changes at the wrong heights:** the tower was scaled, cut, sunk or "
"rotated. Open the file again and leave it as it is."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**The blocks printed slowly:** check the Volumetric flow rate view ([the "
"test flow](page:temperature-tower#flow)). If it shows much less than 10 "
"mm³/s, the file was imported instead of opened (no `M220` markers), or your "
"filament's **Slow down if layer print time is below** is above 28 s: lower "
"it for this print only, without saving."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/troubleshooting"
msgid ""
"**The next print runs far too fast:** the tower was cancelled before its "
"top, and the speed factor is still set. Send `M220 S100` (Klipper: or "
"`RESTART`)."
msgstr ""

#. step title
msgctxt "temperature-tower/troubleshooting-more"
msgid "Other problems"
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**An error or the wrong tool heating at a block change (IDEX):** use the "
"file for the tool the object is assigned to."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**\"Temperature out of range\":** do not raise the firmware limit; use the "
"fine file or a cooler range."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**The printer stops with an error at a block change** (for example \"heater "
"not found\", or a Klipper error about an extruder): on an IDEX or "
"multi-tool printer you printed a file for another tool. Use the file for "
"the tool the object is assigned to."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**The other tool on an IDEX printer heats or cools:** the object is "
"assigned to the wrong extruder. In the object list, set its extruder to the "
"one the file is for."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**\"Temperature out of range\" / above maximum:** the hottest blocks are "
"above your hotend's limit. Do not raise the firmware limit for this test: "
"it exists to protect the hotend. Use the fine file if only the coarse one "
"is too hot."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**Everything fails hot, even the top block:** check that the part cooling "
"fan works and is on. If it is, the best temperature may be below the "
"tower's range: take the coldest block that passes the snap flag and check "
"the manufacturer's range."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**No flag passes:** wet filament, an extruder that under-extrudes (do the "
"first-layer and extruder checks), or a speed far above the hotend's melting "
"capacity (see the [maximum volumetric flow](page:max-flow) test)."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**The tower came loose or a ladder post broke off:** clean the bed, check "
"the first layer, and use a brim. Score the damaged features –."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "temperature-tower/troubleshooting-more"
msgid ""
"**PrusaSlicer 3.x alpha:** not tested with these files yet. PrusaSlicer 3.0 "
"also has its own built-in temperature tower in its Calibration menu."
msgstr ""

#. scorecard title
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment"
msgid "IDEX alignment card"
msgstr ""

#. scorecard intro
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment"
msgid "One row per station, X and Y readings in mm."
msgstr ""

#. scorecard rule_title
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment"
msgid "How the card works"
msgstr ""

#. scoring rule
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/rules"
msgid ""
"Choose the **bed preset** of the file you printed and the **test** (Vernier "
"or Lite). The card knows where every station sits on each published bed "
"preset."
msgstr ""

#. scoring rule
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/rules"
msgid ""
"Read every station **on the bed, before removing the print**. Stations are "
"numbered like a phone keypad **seen from the front**: 1 back-left, 5 "
"centre, 9 front-right."
msgstr ""

#. scoring rule
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/rules"
msgid ""
"**Vernier:** write the **fine** reading in mm with its sign: a labelled "
"tick is that many tenths (\"2\" = 0.20), the unlabelled tick just beyond a "
"label adds 0.05, and a reading on the **\"+\" side** is positive. Fill the "
"**coarse** box only when the coarse scale reads 6, 8 or 10, or when the "
"fine scale has no clear alignment: the card then picks the right repeat of "
"the fine scale."
msgstr ""

#. scoring rule
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/rules"
msgid ""
"**Lite:** type **+** when T1 moved towards + (X: gap on the left, overlap "
"on the right; Y: gap at the front, overlap at the back), **−** for the "
"opposite, **0** when both contact lines look the same. Lite gives a sign "
"pattern only, no numbers."
msgstr ""

#. scoring rule
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/rules"
msgid ""
"The card fits a plane through the readings of each axis, **reading = c0 + "
"cx·x + cy·y**, with x and y in mm from the bed centre. c0 is the tool "
"offset at the bed centre; the slopes show the mechanics."
msgstr ""

#. scoring rule
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/rules"
msgid ""
"Each slope is judged by its **effect at the outer stations**: **ok** below "
"0.05 mm (one fine step) or within the reading noise, **watch** from 0.05 to "
"0.10 mm, **fix** above 0.10 mm. The offset is either ok (below 0.05 mm) or "
"worth applying."
msgstr ""

#. scoring rule
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/rules"
msgid ""
"A station more than **0.10 mm off the plane** is flagged: re-read it with a "
"loupe before trusting the result."
msgstr ""

#. scoring rule
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/rules"
msgid ""
"**Fix the mechanics first** (any slope marked fix), print again, then set "
"the offset. Every correction is **new = old + reading**. On RatOS, do the "
"[sign check](page:idex-alignment#sign-check) once before trusting it."
msgstr ""

#. feature name
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/x0"
msgid "X offset"
msgstr ""

#. feature pattern
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/x0"
msgid "Same X reading at every station"
msgstr ""

#. feature cause
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/x0"
msgid "Plain X tool offset"
msgstr ""

#. feature action
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/x0"
msgid "Apply the X correction below (new = old + reading)."
msgstr ""

#. feature name
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/y0"
msgid "Y offset"
msgstr ""

#. feature pattern
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/y0"
msgid "Same Y reading at every station"
msgstr ""

#. feature cause
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/y0"
msgid "Plain Y tool offset"
msgstr ""

#. feature action
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/y0"
msgid "Apply the Y correction below (new = old + reading)."
msgstr ""

#. feature name
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/dx_dx"
msgid "X scale (X reading changes from left to right)"
msgstr ""

#. feature pattern
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/dx_dx"
msgid "X reading changes from left to right"
msgstr ""

#. feature cause
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/dx_dx"
msgid ""
"The two carriages travel different distances in X: belt tension or pitch, a "
"pulley, or the X `rotation_distance` of one carriage."
msgstr ""

#. feature action
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/dx_dx"
msgid ""
"Check both X belts (same tension, same belt), the pulley set screws and the "
"motor mounts. If T0 measures right on a dimensional test, set T1's "
"rotation_distance to old × (1 + slope)."
msgstr ""

#. feature name
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/dy_dx"
msgid "Rails or gantry (Y reading changes from left to right)"
msgstr ""

#. feature pattern
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/dy_dx"
msgid "Y reading changes from left to right"
msgstr ""

#. feature cause
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/dy_dx"
msgid ""
"T1's X travel is not parallel to T0's: carriage rails not parallel, or "
"gantry twist or flex that depends on where the parked carriage sits."
msgstr ""

#. feature action
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/dy_dx"
msgid ""
"Check that the X rail(s) are straight and bolted down, and that the gantry "
"is not twisted. A pure rotation can also be a loose toolhead mount."
msgstr ""

#. feature vc4
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/dy_dx"
msgid ""
"**RatRig V-Core 4/4.1 IDEX** (field experience, the author's own printer): "
"a Y gap that grows across the bed was fixed by making the two toolhead "
"belts the **same length**. Count the teeth, or compare the belt ends at the "
"clamps: 1–2 teeth of difference are enough. Re-tension both equally, then "
"print the Vernier again to confirm."
msgstr ""

#. feature name
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/dx_dy"
msgid "Racking (X reading changes from front to back)"
msgstr ""

#. feature pattern
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/dx_dy"
msgid "X reading changes from front to back"
msgstr ""

#. feature cause
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/dx_dy"
msgid ""
"Yaw or racking of the gantry as it moves in Y, seen differently by the two "
"carriages."
msgstr ""

#. feature action
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/dx_dy"
msgid ""
"Square the gantry on both Y sides, compare the left and right Y belt "
"tension, and check the gantry joints."
msgstr ""

#. feature name
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/dy_dy"
msgid "Y scale (Y reading changes from front to back)"
msgstr ""

#. feature pattern
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/dy_dy"
msgid "Y reading changes from front to back"
msgstr ""

#. feature cause
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/dy_dy"
msgid ""
"Rare with a shared Y gantry: usually a misreading, a loose nozzle or "
"hotend, or the bed moving between the two tools."
msgstr ""

#. feature action
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/dy_dy"
msgid "Re-read the stations; check that both hotends and the bed are rigid."
msgstr ""

#. feature vc4
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/dy_dy"
msgid ""
"**RatRig V-Core 4/4.1 IDEX:** the same belt-length check as for a Y reading "
"that changes from left to right can apply here (field experience, the "
"author's own printer)."
msgstr ""

#. feature name
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/residual"
msgid "One station off the plane"
msgstr ""

#. feature pattern
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/residual"
msgid "One station disagrees with the plane through the others"
msgstr ""

#. feature cause
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/residual"
msgid ""
"A misreading, a bad first layer at that spot (bed mesh), or local backlash."
msgstr ""

#. feature action
msgctxt "features/idex-alignment/residual"
msgid ""
"Re-read that station with a loupe; if it persists, check the bed mesh and "
"the carriage there."
msgstr ""

#. scorecard title
msgctxt "features/max-flow"
msgid "Maximum volumetric flow scorecard"
msgstr ""

#. scorecard intro
msgctxt "features/max-flow"
msgid "One row per band: the wall, the heater, the extruder."
msgstr ""

#. scorecard rule_title
msgctxt "features/max-flow"
msgid "How the limit is chosen"
msgstr ""

#. scoring rule
msgctxt "features/max-flow/rules"
msgid ""
"Read **from the bottom up**, on the **middle of the two long straights, "
"both walls**, in the **upper two thirds** of each band (its first layers "
"are the transition)."
msgstr ""

#. scoring rule
msgctxt "features/max-flow/rules"
msgid ""
"Mark the wall of each band: **✓** pass (even, complete, same gloss as "
"below), **◐** degrading (matte or satin, the odd thin line, lines still "
"complete), **✕** fail (gaps, missing or stringy lines, light through the "
"wall), **–** not scored."
msgstr ""

#. scoring rule
msgctxt "features/max-flow/rules"
msgid ""
"Tick **Heater sag** if the hotend temperature sagged more than about 3 °C "
"during that band, and **Clicks** if the extruder clicked or knocked. Either "
"one counts as a fail, even if the wall still looks fine."
msgstr ""

#. scoring rule
msgctxt "features/max-flow/rules"
msgid ""
"The **onset** is the lowest band that fails. The **limit** is the band just "
"below it."
msgstr ""

#. scoring rule
msgctxt "features/max-flow/rules"
msgid ""
"Set the filament's **Max volumetric speed** to **85 %** of the limit (**80 "
"%** if the limit band was already degrading; up to **90 %** for a clean, "
"sharp onset)."
msgstr ""

#. scoring rule
msgctxt "features/max-flow/rules"
msgid ""
"**Band 1 already fails:** not a flow problem (temperature far too low, "
"clog, wet filament, wrong filament diameter). **Nothing fails:** print the "
"next file up for your nozzle."
msgstr ""

#. scoring rule
msgctxt "features/max-flow/rules"
msgid ""
"**IDEX:** one card per tool. Write the tool, nozzle and temperature on it."
msgstr ""

#. feature name
msgctxt "features/max-flow/faces"
msgid "Middle of the long straights"
msgstr ""

#. feature what
msgctxt "features/max-flow/faces"
msgid ""
"The **middle third of both long straights**, outside and inside, where the "
"nozzle holds full speed. The column and the round end do not count."
msgstr ""

#. feature pass
msgctxt "features/max-flow/faces"
msgid ""
"A continuous, even wall: every line touches the next, with the same gloss "
"as the low bands."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail
msgctxt "features/max-flow/faces"
msgid ""
"Thin or missing lines, gaps between layers, a rough \"sandpaper\" texture "
"or stringy lines. It starts in the middle of the straights and spreads in "
"the next bands."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "features/max-flow/faces"
msgid ""
"Hold the part at arm's length first, then close up with a light raking "
"along each straight. The straight along X faces the back: turn the part "
"round."
msgstr ""

#. photo alt
msgctxt "features/max-flow/faces"
msgid ""
"Close-up of the middle of the front straight, bands 14 to 30 of the yellow "
"PETG wedge: fine, even layer lines, the wall closed and smooth"
msgstr ""

#. photo caption
msgctxt "features/max-flow/faces"
msgid ""
"Bands 14–30 (hf-0.4, PETG, T0): every line complete, the wall closed and "
"smooth."
msgstr ""

#. photo alt
msgctxt "features/max-flow/faces"
msgid ""
"Close-up of the same part of the front straight, bands 34 to 50: thin, "
"broken and missing lines, loose strings and small blobs"
msgstr ""

#. photo caption
msgctxt "features/max-flow/faces"
msgid ""
"Bands 34–50, same photo, same spot along the straight: thin and broken "
"lines, gaps and strings. Band 34 is the onset."
msgstr ""

#. More: intro
msgctxt "features/max-flow/faces"
msgid ""
"The engraved number is the flow **on the long straights**: the nozzle "
"speeds up out of the column, holds full speed along the straight, keeps "
"most of it through the round end and brakes back into the column. So judge "
"**the middle third of each straight**, in the **upper two thirds** of each "
"band: the first layers of a band are the transition while the hotend "
"settles at the new flow."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "features/max-flow/faces"
msgid ""
"Find the **lowest band that fails**, not the worst-looking one. The band "
"just below it is your limit."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "features/max-flow/faces"
msgid ""
"**The divider groove is not a defect.** At the bottom of every band a "
"groove runs across the numbers face, and the first layers change. Judge "
"each band above it."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "features/max-flow/faces"
msgid ""
"**The seam is not a failure.** The seam is on the column's rear corner, "
"away from the straights you read: a small zit or gap there means nothing "
"here."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "features/max-flow/faces"
msgid ""
"**Wavy lines near the start of a straight** are ringing (vibration) after "
"the corner, not flow. They fade along the straight and look the same in "
"many bands."
msgstr ""

#. feature name
msgctxt "features/max-flow/light"
msgid "See-through test"
msgstr ""

#. feature what
msgctxt "features/max-flow/light"
msgid ""
"Hold the part against a lamp or a bright window, or shine a phone torch "
"inside the wedge, at one straight at a time."
msgstr ""

#. feature pass
msgctxt "features/max-flow/light"
msgid "Light shows evenly through the wall: no bright dots, no slits."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail
msgctxt "features/max-flow/light"
msgid ""
"Bright pinholes or horizontal slits along the straights. The first band "
"with them is the onset."
msgstr ""

#. More: intro
msgctxt "features/max-flow/light"
msgid ""
"The wall is a single line, so under-extrusion lets light through long "
"before it is obvious from outside. In a dark room the failing bands light "
"up."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "features/max-flow/light"
msgid ""
"Dark filament shows little; a light colour works best. With black filament, "
"rely on the straights and the squeeze test."
msgstr ""

#. feature name
msgctxt "features/max-flow/sheen"
msgid "Sheen (early cue)"
msgstr ""

#. feature what
msgctxt "features/max-flow/sheen"
msgid ""
"The **gloss** of the long straights, band by band. PETG is glossy when the "
"flow is fine."
msgstr ""

#. feature pass
msgctxt "features/max-flow/sheen"
msgid "The same gloss as the bands below."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail
msgctxt "features/max-flow/sheen"
msgid ""
"The wall turns **matte or satin** while the lines are still complete: you "
"are close to the limit, usually one band before the onset."
msgstr ""

#. More: intro
msgctxt "features/max-flow/sheen"
msgid ""
"As the flow rises, the plastic leaves the nozzle colder, and the surface "
"loses its gloss. It is an **early cue**: mark that band **◐ degrading** on "
"the card."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "features/max-flow/sheen"
msgid ""
"Some filaments (matte PLA, silk, carbon-filled) never shine: skip this cue "
"for them."
msgstr ""

#. feature name
msgctxt "features/max-flow/corners"
msgid "Column vs straights (diagnosis)"
msgstr ""

#. feature what
msgctxt "features/max-flow/corners"
msgid "The hex column, compared with the long straights of the same band."
msgstr ""

#. feature pass
msgctxt "features/max-flow/corners"
msgid ""
"When the straights fail, the column still looks solid: the nozzle nearly "
"stops at its corners, so this is a true melt (flow) limit."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail
msgctxt "features/max-flow/corners"
msgid ""
"The column fails in the same band as the straights, or before them: suspect "
"something else (extruder skipping, temperature too low, a partial clog, or "
"the slicer slowing the layer down)."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "features/max-flow/corners"
msgid ""
"On a slow-accelerating printer (1000 mm/s² and below) the fast 0.4 files "
"hold the top band's speed for only 0.1–0.2 s on the straights. Print the "
"larger-nozzle file of your tier if you can: same flow, lower speed."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "features/max-flow/corners"
msgid ""
"The round end slows the fastest bands a little (above about 140–170 mm/s). "
"It is not read; if it looks better than the straights, that is why."
msgstr ""

#. feature name
msgctxt "features/max-flow/clicks"
msgid "Extruder clicks (listen while printing)"
msgstr ""

#. feature what
msgctxt "features/max-flow/clicks"
msgid ""
"Listen to the extruder while the upper bands print, and note the number on "
"the column at the nozzle height."
msgstr ""

#. feature pass
msgctxt "features/max-flow/clicks"
msgid "No clicks."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail
msgctxt "features/max-flow/clicks"
msgid ""
"A click or knock: the gear slipped on the filament. That band is at or "
"above the limit, even if the wall still looks acceptable."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "features/max-flow/clicks"
msgid "Tick **Clicks** on the card for that band."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "features/max-flow/clicks"
msgid ""
"Stay close to the printer during the top bands. A phone held close to the "
"extruder records the sound if you want to check it later."
msgstr ""

#. feature name
msgctxt "features/max-flow/heater"
msgid "Heater sag (watch the temperature)"
msgstr ""

#. feature what
msgctxt "features/max-flow/heater"
msgid ""
"The hotend temperature graph (Mainsail, Fluidd, OctoPrint or the printer's "
"screen) during the upper bands."
msgstr ""

#. feature pass
msgctxt "features/max-flow/heater"
msgid ""
"The temperature stays within about **2–3 °C** of the target through the "
"band."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail
msgctxt "features/max-flow/heater"
msgid ""
"It drops further and recovers only after the band, or Klipper stops with "
"\"Heater extruder not heating at expected rate\": the heater cannot melt "
"that flow. That band is at or above the limit."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "features/max-flow/heater"
msgid "Tick **Heater sag** on the card for the first band where it sags."
msgstr ""

#. point (warn)
msgctxt "features/max-flow/heater"
msgid ""
"Never raise the temperature above the hotend's or the filament's rated "
"maximum to chase a higher band."
msgstr ""

#. More: intro
msgctxt "features/max-flow/heater"
msgid ""
"Melting 50 mm³/s and more takes a lot of heater power. When the heater "
"cannot keep up, the hotend cools, and the melt with it: the wall fails a "
"band or two later."
msgstr ""

#. feature name
msgctxt "features/max-flow/bottleneck"
msgid "Extruder or hotend? (diagnosis)"
msgstr ""

#. feature what
msgctxt "features/max-flow/bottleneck"
msgid ""
"Compare the clicks with the wall of the same band and of the band below."
msgstr ""

#. feature pass
msgctxt "features/max-flow/bottleneck"
msgid ""
"Thin, rough wall first, clicks later or never: the **hotend's** melt limit."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail
msgctxt "features/max-flow/bottleneck"
msgid ""
"Clicks while the wall of that band is still complete: the **extruder** is "
"the limit (grip, motor torque or speed)."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "features/max-flow/bottleneck"
msgid "Either way, the band below is your usable limit."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "features/max-flow/bottleneck"
msgid ""
"1 mm/s of 1.75 mm filament is 2.4 mm³/s. Above about 50–70 mm³/s, "
"pancake-motor extruders (Orbiter, Sherpa Mini, Galileo 2, LGX Lite) often "
"slip before a UHF hotend gives up. More motor current (within its rating), "
"a stronger extruder or a hotter melt that needs less force can raise the "
"extruder limit; nothing on the extruder helps a hotend limit."
msgstr ""

#. feature name
msgctxt "features/max-flow/squeeze"
msgid "Layer bond (squeeze test)"
msgstr ""

#. feature what
msgctxt "features/max-flow/squeeze"
msgid ""
"After printing, squeeze a long straight gently between thumb and finger, "
"band by band, from the bottom up."
msgstr ""

#. feature pass
msgctxt "features/max-flow/squeeze"
msgid "The wall flexes and springs back."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail
msgctxt "features/max-flow/squeeze"
msgid ""
"The wall cracks or splits along a layer line: the layers did not fuse at "
"that flow."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "features/max-flow/squeeze"
msgid ""
"Do it **last**, after every other reading and after any photo: a cracked "
"band cannot be looked at again."
msgstr ""

#. feature name
msgctxt "features/max-flow/label"
msgid "Flow numbers on the column"
msgstr ""

#. feature what
msgctxt "features/max-flow/label"
msgid ""
"The column's front-left face is doubled (thicker) and carries one number "
"per band: its flow in mm³/s on the long straights."
msgstr ""

#. feature pass
msgctxt "features/max-flow/label"
msgid ""
"The numbers are readable all the way up: the numbers face prints at half "
"speed, with ordinary lines, so it stays readable above the onset."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail
msgctxt "features/max-flow/label"
msgid ""
"Unreadable from the start: the file was imported, not opened as a project. "
"Check the layer height in the preview."
msgstr ""

#. photo alt
msgctxt "features/max-flow/label"
msgid ""
"Close-up of the column's numbers face, 50 at the top down to 18, with the "
"start of the front straight beside it: the wall is smooth level with 30 and "
"below, torn with gaps level with 34 and above"
msgstr ""

#. photo caption
msgctxt "features/max-flow/label"
msgid ""
"The numbers face stays readable all the way up, while the straight beside "
"it is already torn: smooth level with 30, gaps from 34 up."
msgstr ""

#. scorecard title
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance"
msgid "Pressure advance scorecard"
msgstr ""

#. scorecard intro
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance"
msgid "One row per band, one column per probe."
msgstr ""

#. scorecard rule_title
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance"
msgid "How the best band is chosen"
msgstr ""

#. scoring rule
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/rules"
msgid ""
"Hold the tower with the engraved band numbers facing you. Score each band "
"on its own, **in its middle**, away from the divider groove: **✓** pass, "
"**L** fails because PA is too low (bulge, blob, filled corner), **H** fails "
"because PA is too high (rounded, gap, slit), **–** cannot judge."
msgstr ""

#. scoring rule
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/rules"
msgid ""
"Go up from band 1. A band **qualifies** when both **90° tips** are ✓ and "
"**no counted probe** is H. The **Inside** column is not counted: a concave "
"corner is rounded by the bead width in every band, so do not judge PA there."
msgstr ""

#. scoring rule
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/rules"
msgid ""
"Your value is the **lowest band that qualifies**. The 45° tip may still be "
"L there: that is normal."
msgstr ""

#. scoring rule
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/rules"
msgid ""
"**Tie:** if the band just above also qualifies and has at least as many ✓, "
"take that **higher** band (when in doubt, lean higher)."
msgstr ""

#. scoring rule
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/rules"
msgid ""
"If the result is **band 1 or the top band**, the value may lie outside the "
"range: print the other file of your firmware (coarse after fine, fine after "
"coarse)."
msgstr ""

#. scoring rule
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/rules"
msgid ""
"**Sanity check:** L marks belong at the bottom (low PA) and H marks at the "
"top. Marks scattered at random, or L and H on the same probe of one band, "
"point to something else: see "
"[Troubleshooting](page:pressure-advance#troubleshooting)."
msgstr ""

#. feature name
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip90"
msgid "90° blade tips"
msgstr ""

#. feature short
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip90"
msgid "90° tips"
msgstr ""

#. feature what
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip90"
msgid ""
"**Right tip** (end of the front-right face) and **back-left tip** (end of "
"the back face): the main reading. Follow each one from band 1 up, under "
"raking light."
msgstr ""

#. feature pass
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip90"
msgid ""
"Both tips are sharp, straight vertical edges. The two faces meet in a crisp "
"line, with no swelling and no notch."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail_low
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip90"
msgid ""
"The tip bulges outwards: a rounded, swollen edge or a vertical row of small "
"blobs, often with a slight bump just before it on the long face."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail_high
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip90"
msgid ""
"The tip is rounded off or \"eaten\": a dull, sunken edge. The last 1–2 mm "
"before the tip look thin or gappy."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip90"
msgid "Mark ✓ only when **both** tips pass."
msgstr ""

#. photo alt
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip90"
msgid ""
"The coarse PETG pressure advance tower in silhouette against a light panel, "
"band numbers 1 to 9 on the front: the outline of the tip on the right is "
"straight in the low bands and ragged from about band 6 up"
msgstr ""

#. photo caption
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip90"
msgid ""
"Against a light background the tip's outline is easy to read. Author's "
"coarse PETG tower (PA 0 → 0.20): straight up to band 5, ragged from band 6 "
"up, where PA is too high and eats the tip. With PA too low the same outline "
"would be bumpy in the lowest bands instead."
msgstr ""

#. More: intro
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip90"
msgid ""
"**Two tips:** the **right blade**, at the end of the long front-right face, "
"and the **back-left blade**, at the end of the back face. The nozzle "
"arrives at full speed along a straight, brakes hard and turns 90°. These "
"convex tips are **the main reading**: they behave like the 90° corners of "
"your real parts, and PA shows on them first."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip90"
msgid ""
"**Follow one tip from band 1 upward**, with the light raking along the "
"face. With PA too low its edge carries a **vertical row of small blobs**; "
"the row fades out at the right band. Then check the other tip."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip90"
msgid ""
"Look straight down the tip edge, as if sighting along a ruler. Then look "
"from the side and imagine a set square on the tip."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip90"
msgid ""
"Mark ✓ only when **both** tips pass. If one bulges and the other does not, "
"mark L: the back-left tip has a shorter straight, so it often reacts a "
"little later."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip90"
msgid ""
"**Look at the tip edge-on against a light background** (a window, a sheet "
"of white paper), or photograph it that way: the blobs stand out as a bumpy "
"outline, and the band where the outline turns straight is easy to spot."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip90"
msgid ""
"Compare with band 1 first. It is printed with PA off, so its tips show you "
"exactly what \"bulge\" looks like on your printer. Then go up until the "
"tips look like the corner of a sheet of paper."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip90"
msgid ""
"The reading is valid for the test speed (98 mm/s) and your "
"external-perimeter acceleration (and, on Klipper, "
"`square_corner_velocity`). Write them down with the result: a different "
"acceleration moves the optimum."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip90"
msgid ""
"**Every band looks identical:** PA effects are small at low speed. Reprint "
"faster with `--flow 12` (about 147 mm/s) or at your real external-perimeter "
"speed ([the test speed](page:pressure-advance#speed)), staying below 0.8 × "
"your filament's max volumetric speed."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip90"
msgid ""
"**Wrong temperature:** plastic that is too hot also swells corners, in "
"every band. If even the top band bulges, check that you printed at the "
"temperature chosen with the [temperature tower](page:temperature-tower) "
"before raising PA."
msgstr ""

#. More: photo alt
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip90"
msgid ""
"The right 90 degree tip of the coarse PETG tower around band 3 (PA 0.050), "
"seen from outside the tip: a straight, sharp edge between two even faces"
msgstr ""

#. More: photo caption
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip90"
msgid ""
"Coarse tower, band 3 (PA 0.050) in the middle of the frame: the right tip "
"is a straight, sharp edge and the front-right face, on the left, stays even "
"right up to it. Bands 2 and 4 look the same."
msgstr ""

#. More: photo alt
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip90"
msgid ""
"The same right 90 degree tip around band 8 (PA 0.175): the edge eaten away "
"in scallops and the end of the front-right face gouged and thin"
msgstr ""

#. More: photo caption
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip90"
msgid ""
"Same photo and framing, band 8 (PA 0.175), clearly too high: the tip is "
"eaten away in scallops and the last millimetre of the front-right face "
"before it is thin and gappy. On this tower the damage starts faintly in "
"band 5 (0.100) and is plain from band 7 (0.150) up."
msgstr ""

#. feature name
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip45"
msgid "45° blade tip"
msgstr ""

#. feature short
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip45"
msgid "45° tip"
msgstr ""

#. feature what
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip45"
msgid ""
"**Front-left blade tip**, at the end of the 18 mm front-left face: the most "
"sensitive probe."
msgstr ""

#. feature pass
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip45"
msgid "A thin, clean point. Both faces stay flat right up to the tip."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail_low
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip45"
msgid ""
"The point is fat or club-shaped, with a blob or a seam-like ridge on the "
"tip."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail_high
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip45"
msgid ""
"The point is blunt, short or ragged. A small gap or hollow shows on the tip "
"or just before it."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip45"
msgid ""
"Compare its point with the bands above and below, rather than judging it "
"alone."
msgstr ""

#. More: intro
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip45"
msgid ""
"**Front-left blade,** at the end of the long front-left face (18 mm). The "
"nozzle almost stops here before turning back, so this is the **most "
"sensitive** probe: the last to stop bulging as PA rises, and the first to "
"blunt when it is too high."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip45"
msgid ""
"It is normal for the 45° tip to still swell **slightly** in the band where "
"the 90° tips already pass. Do not raise PA just for it."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip45"
msgid ""
"Use it as the early warning for too high: when it starts to look blunt or "
"gapped, the band above your choice is already too much."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/tip45"
msgid ""
"A blob on the tip in **every** band, the same size, is usually not PA: look "
"at the seam settings, or at a wipe or retraction move ending there."
msgstr ""

#. feature name
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/inner"
msgid "Inside corners"
msgstr ""

#. feature short
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/inner"
msgid "Inside"
msgstr ""

#. feature what
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/inner"
msgid ""
"**Three inside corners**, where each blade folds back onto the hexagon. "
"**Not counted:** do not judge PA here."
msgstr ""

#. feature pass
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/inner"
msgid ""
"A slightly rounded crease, the same in every band. That is normal at any PA."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail_low
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/inner"
msgid ""
"Do not mark L here: a filled, rounded crease is the width of the bead, not "
"PA too low."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail_high
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/inner"
msgid ""
"A dark slit or gap opens in the crease, only in the top bands. It confirms "
"an H on the tips; it never decides alone."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/inner"
msgid ""
"Glance at them last, after the tips. If the crease looks the same in every "
"band, that is what it should do: move on."
msgstr ""

#. More: intro
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/inner"
msgid ""
"**Three inside corners,** where each blade folds back onto the hexagon: on "
"the **back-right face** (behind the right blade), on the **back-left "
"face**, and on the **front face**, left of the numbers (behind the 45° "
"blade). **Do not judge PA here, and they are not counted.** On the outside "
"of a print a concave corner is always slightly rounded, by about half a "
"line width: the bead cannot form a sharp inward crease, whatever the PA."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/inner"
msgid ""
"**\"The inside corners are filled in every band, so PA must go higher.\"** "
"No: a concave corner is always rounded by the bead width. On the first real "
"print they looked the same in all nine bands while the tips had already "
"found the value (0.04). Read the convex tips instead."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/inner"
msgid ""
"A slit in **every** band, also in band 1, is not PA too high: it is "
"under-extrusion or a gap between the two wall lines. Check the flow and the "
"filament diameter."
msgstr ""

#. feature name
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/straight"
msgid "Long straights (speed change)"
msgstr ""

#. feature short
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/straight"
msgid "Straights"
msgstr ""

#. feature what
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/straight"
msgid ""
"**Front-right face** (15 mm) and **front-left face** (18 mm): compare the "
"line width at both ends, in light that skims along the face."
msgstr ""

#. feature pass
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/straight"
msgid "Even line width and sheen along the whole face."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail_low
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/straight"
msgid ""
"The first 3–5 mm after the plain hex corner look thin or matte (the "
"pressure lags). The last few millimetres before the tip look swollen."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail_high
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/straight"
msgid ""
"The start of the face looks slightly swollen. The end before the tip looks "
"thin or dull."
msgstr ""

#. More: intro
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/straight"
msgid ""
"**Front-right face** (15 mm, into the right tip) and **front-left face** "
"(18 mm, into the 45° tip). The nozzle speeds up after the previous corner "
"and slows down into the tip: PA shows as a change in line width at both "
"ends of the face."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/straight"
msgid ""
"Hold the face almost edge-on to a lamp, so the light skims along it. Look "
"at the two ends, not the middle. The **last millimetre before the tip** is "
"the most telling: it swells with PA too low and thins with PA too high."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/straight"
msgid ""
"On fast profiles with high acceleration the acceleration phase is short: "
"the effect at the start of the face can be faint. Score what you see; the "
"tips decide."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/straight"
msgid ""
"**Ringing or ghosting is not PA.** Ripples that repeat after each corner "
"and fade along the face are vibrations of the printer: they are fixed with "
"input shaping, not with PA. PA changes the **width** of the line, it does "
"not make waves."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/straight"
msgid ""
"**Wet filament** gives a rough, uneven, sometimes bubbly surface in every "
"band. It mimics the thin starts of PA too low. Dry the spool and print "
"again."
msgstr ""

#. feature name
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/seam"
msgid "Seam: line start and end"
msgstr ""

#. feature short
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/seam"
msgid "Seam"
msgstr ""

#. feature what
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/seam"
msgid ""
"**Rear bump**, in the middle of the back face: every layer starts and ends "
"there. It only **confirms** the corner reading."
msgstr ""

#. feature pass
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/seam"
msgid "A small, even seam line: no blob, no hole."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail_low
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/seam"
msgid ""
"A blob or zit at the end of the line, with a thin or missing start next to "
"it."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail_high
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/seam"
msgid ""
"A gap or hole at the line start or where the line ends; on the top bands of "
"a coarse tower the seam opens clearly."
msgstr ""

#. More: intro
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/seam"
msgid ""
"**Rear bump,** in the middle of the back face: a convex probe. Every layer "
"starts and ends on its apex. Retraction and wipe also act on this spot, so "
"the seam only **confirms** the corner reading: it never decides on its own."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/seam"
msgid ""
"Look at the bump from straight behind, with the light from one side. The "
"seam is the thin vertical line on the top of the bump."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/seam"
msgid ""
"A seam that looks the same in every band is set by retraction, wipe or the "
"seam settings, not by PA. Score it – and move on."
msgstr ""

#. feature name
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/hex120"
msgid "Plain 120° hex corners (reference)"
msgstr ""

#. feature what
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/hex120"
msgid ""
"**Three gentle corners:** front-right (next to the numbers), back-right and "
"left. They should look fine over a wide range of PA: use them as a sanity "
"check."
msgstr ""

#. feature pass
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/hex120"
msgid "Clean in most bands."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail_low
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/hex120"
msgid ""
"Even these corners bulge: PA is far too low (typical of band 1, PA 0)."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail_high
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/hex120"
msgid "Even these corners look dull or gapped: PA is far too high."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/hex120"
msgid ""
"If the 120° corners already fail in the band you are about to choose, your "
"choice is far off: look again at the tips."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "features/pressure-advance/hex120"
msgid ""
"**The divider groove is not a defect.** At the bottom of every band a "
"groove runs across the front face, and the wall changes slightly right "
"where the new value starts. Judge each band in its middle, never at the "
"groove."
msgstr ""

#. scorecard title
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower"
msgid "Temperature tower scorecard"
msgstr ""

#. scorecard intro
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower"
msgid "One row per block, one column per feature."
msgstr ""

#. scorecard rule_title
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower"
msgid "How the best block is chosen"
msgstr ""

#. scoring rule
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/rules"
msgid ""
"Hold the tower with the engraved numbers facing you. Score each block on "
"its own, **above its bottom 1 mm** (there the temperature was still "
"changing): **✓** pass, **H** fails because it is too hot, **C** fails "
"because it is too cold, **–** cannot judge (damaged, not printed, not "
"scored for this material)."
msgstr ""

#. scoring rule
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/rules"
msgid ""
"**Strings first, before you touch the tower:** brushing or handling removes "
"them."
msgstr ""

#. scoring rule
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/rules"
msgid ""
"**Strength first.** A block whose **snap flag** fails is out, however good "
"it looks: a part that splits between layers is never the right answer. "
"(TPU: the flag is not scored; skip this rule.)"
msgstr ""

#. scoring rule
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/rules"
msgid ""
"Among the blocks left, the one with the **most ✓** wins. The **Hole** "
"column is not counted: mark it to see whether it agrees, it never adds a ✓ "
"and never breaks a tie."
msgstr ""

#. scoring rule
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/rules"
msgid "**Tie:** the block with fewer failures (H + C) wins."
msgstr ""

#. scoring rule
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/rules"
msgid "**Still a tie:** the **cooler** block wins."
msgstr ""

#. scoring rule
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/rules"
msgid ""
"**Note:** for parts that must be strong, go **one block hotter** than the "
"block the rules picked."
msgstr ""

#. scoring rule
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/rules"
msgid ""
"**Not every column changes.** At the tower's flow and over a wide range, "
"the strings usually show the hot end, and the smooth face and the top of "
"the bridge show the cold end; the label, the hole and the ramp may look the "
"same on every block. A column that does not change gets ✓ on every block: "
"the flag and the columns that do change decide. The hole is never counted."
msgstr ""

#. scoring rule
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/rules"
msgid ""
"**Sanity check:** H marks belong at the bottom (hot end) and C marks at the "
"top (cold end). An H above a C, or marks scattered at random, means "
"something other than temperature is at work: see "
"[Troubleshooting](page:temperature-tower#troubleshooting)."
msgstr ""

#. feature name
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/pins"
msgid "String ladder (stringing)"
msgstr ""

#. feature short
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/pins"
msgid "Strings"
msgstr ""

#. feature what
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/pins"
msgid ""
"**Back corner:** two small posts tied to the corner by rungs, with an open "
"**window** in every block. Look for strings across the windows. Mostly "
"shows heat."
msgstr ""

#. feature pass
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/pins"
msgid "No strings across the windows, or a few fine hairs that brush off."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail_hot
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/pins"
msgid "Strings or a web across the windows; blobs on the posts."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail_cold
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/pins"
msgid "No strings, but the posts are rough or have gaps between layers."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/pins"
msgid ""
"**Score this first**, before you touch the tower, looking **through** the "
"windows against a bright light."
msgstr ""

#. photo alt
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/pins"
msgid ""
"The string ladder's window on two blocks of the PETG tower: almost clean at "
"210 °C, a dense web of strings at 270 °C"
msgstr ""

#. photo caption
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/pins"
msgid ""
"Top, 210 °C: almost clean. Bottom, 270 °C, too hot: a dense web across the "
"window. At 240 °C, the block this tower picks, a few strings remain (see "
"the whole ladder under More)."
msgstr ""

#. More: intro
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/pins"
msgid ""
"The ladder stands between the back and back-left faces: two small hexagonal "
"posts, each tied to the corner by a short rung at the bottom of each block. "
"Every layer the nozzle travels from the corner to the posts and on to the "
"flag; hot plastic oozes and leaves strings across the windows. Strings to "
"the flag count here too."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/pins"
msgid ""
"**Score this first, before you touch the tower.** Brushing, handling or "
"even a careful look with your fingers removes most strings."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/pins"
msgid ""
"Look **through** the windows from the side, along the back face or along "
"the back-left face, with a lamp or a bright window **behind** the tower: "
"the strings show as dark threads against the light."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/pins"
msgid ""
"Photograph the ladder before brushing too: in the author's tests the "
"strings were obvious on the nozzle cam and in photos taken before brushing, "
"and gone afterwards."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/pins"
msgid ""
"Retraction is not tuned yet at this point of the sequence. Do not chase "
"zero strings: mark H only when the strings are clearly worse than on the "
"colder blocks."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/pins"
msgid ""
"If **every** block strings about the same, the filament is probably wet. "
"Dry it before trusting this column."
msgstr ""

#. More: photo alt
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/pins"
msgid ""
"The whole PETG tower seen from the back-right, the string ladder on the "
"right: strings dense on the bottom blocks, thinning out towards the top"
msgstr ""

#. More: photo caption
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/pins"
msgid ""
"The whole ladder, 270 °C at the bottom to 200 °C at the top: the web thins "
"out block by block. Mark H where it is clearly worse than on the cooler "
"blocks, not wherever a string remains."
msgstr ""

#. More: photo alt
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/pins"
msgid ""
"Two nozzle-cam clips of the string ladder side by side, same framing, half "
"speed, the same moment of a layer: on the left the 270 °C block, strings "
"pulled between the two posts; on the right the 210 °C block, the same move "
"almost clean"
msgstr ""

#. More: photo caption
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/pins"
msgid ""
"Nozzle cam, the author's PETG on T0, half speed. Left, 270 °C: the move "
"between the posts pulls strings across the window. Right, 210 °C: the same "
"move, only a stray hair."
msgstr ""

#. feature name
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/ramp"
msgid "Overhang ramp (55/65/75/80°)"
msgstr ""

#. feature short
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/ramp"
msgid "Ramp"
msgstr ""

#. feature what
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/ramp"
msgid ""
"**Back-right face:** four overhang tongues, at 55°, 65°, 75° and 80°. Note "
"the **steepest tongue whose underside is still clean**. Mostly shows heat."
msgstr ""

#. feature pass
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/ramp"
msgid ""
"The undersides are smooth and the edges straight up to the steepest tongue "
"that is clean on the cooler blocks."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail_hot
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/ramp"
msgid ""
"Undersides droopy or stringy, edges curling up, starting at a lower angle "
"than in cooler blocks."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail_cold
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/ramp"
msgid "Undersides clean, but the lines separate or the edges are ragged."
msgstr ""

#. More: intro
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/ramp"
msgid ""
"55° is next to the right corner, then 65° and 75°, and 80° at the back. "
"They all reach 5 mm out, so only the angle changes: at 75° and 80° each "
"layer hangs almost entirely in the air, where hot plastic droops. Write the "
"angle in the Notes column (55, 65, 75, 80, or 0 if none)."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/ramp"
msgid ""
"Turn the tower so you look up at the undersides of the tongues under a "
"lamp. Start from the 55° tongue next to the right corner, go towards the "
"back and stop at the first rough one."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/ramp"
msgid ""
"The tongues print at the bridge speed (25 mm/s) with the bridge fan: the "
"file turns on PrusaSlicer's *Detect bridging perimeters* for this object. "
"The angle reached also depends on your fan; use the number to compare "
"blocks: the angle at which the tongues start to fail drops as the "
"temperature rises."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/ramp"
msgid ""
"The 80° tongue is very flat and may droop a little on every block. That "
"says nothing about temperature: compare where the droop **starts** from "
"block to block."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/ramp"
msgid ""
"Seen from the side, the tongues may look the same on every block: on the "
"author's PETG tower (270 → 200 °C) they did. Judge the undersides; if they "
"too look the same, mark ✓ on every block."
msgstr ""

#. feature name
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/bridge"
msgid "Long bridge (22 mm beam)"
msgstr ""

#. feature short
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/bridge"
msgid "Bridge"
msgstr ""

#. feature what
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/bridge"
msgid ""
"**In front of the front-left face:** a 22 mm bridge beam on two long ribs. "
"Read it from the side and from below. Fails both ways."
msgstr ""

#. feature pass
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/bridge"
msgid ""
"The underside is flat and straight, the lines touch each other, at most a "
"slight sag."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail_hot
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/bridge"
msgid ""
"Visible sag (more than about 0.5 mm at mid-span) or strands hanging from it."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail_cold
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/bridge"
msgid ""
"The lines do not fuse: gaps between strands, a rough top with lifted "
"strands, lines broken or lifted at the ends."
msgstr ""

#. photo alt
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/bridge"
msgid ""
"The bridge beam of two blocks seen square to the front-left face: rough and "
"lumpy on top at 210 °C, even lines at 240 °C"
msgstr ""

#. photo caption
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/bridge"
msgid ""
"Top, 210 °C, too cold: the top of the beam is rough, with lifted strands "
"and a blob. Bottom, 240 °C, the chosen block: even lines and a clean top "
"edge."
msgstr ""

#. More: intro
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/bridge"
msgid ""
"The ribs stand one at each end of the face; the beam has 5.8 mm of open air "
"under it and 7 mm of open space behind it. Hot plastic sags, cold plastic "
"does not fuse."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/bridge"
msgid ""
"From the side: look square at the front-left face at beam height, with the "
"lamp lighting the face **behind** the beam. The beam's bottom edge then "
"reads as a line against the lit face: compare it with the beam's straight "
"top edge and with the beams of the blocks above and below. From below: tilt "
"the tower back and look up at the underside, with the light skimming along "
"the strands."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/bridge"
msgid ""
"The span is long on purpose: sag grows quickly with span, so a 22 mm beam "
"separates the hot blocks more clearly than a short bridge."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/bridge"
msgid ""
"Look at the **top** of each beam too. On the author's PETG tower (270 → 200 "
"°C) the cold blocks showed there first: from 230 °C down the top was rough, "
"with lifted strands; 240 °C was the cleanest."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/bridge"
msgid ""
"Bridges depend strongly on the bridge fan speed and the bridge flow ratio. "
"If every bridge looks the same, it tells you little about temperature: "
"score them all ✓ or all –."
msgstr ""

#. feature name
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/label"
msgid "Engraved temperature (fine detail)"
msgstr ""

#. feature short
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/label"
msgid "Label"
msgstr ""

#. feature what
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/label"
msgid ""
"**Front face:** the engraved block temperature. Check that the grooves stay "
"crisp. Only confirms."
msgstr ""

#. feature pass
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/label"
msgid ""
"All three digits crisp; the grooves open and sharp-edged along their whole "
"length."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail_hot
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/label"
msgid "Grooves partly filled or blurred, edges rounded, digits hard to read."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail_cold
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/label"
msgid ""
"Grooves fine, but the face around them is matte or rough, or shows gaps "
"between lines."
msgstr ""

#. More: intro
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/label"
msgid ""
"The temperature is centred on the face, engraved with 0.6 mm grooves. It "
"tells you which block you are looking at; as a test it only confirms (it "
"rarely changes from block to block). Mostly shows heat."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/label"
msgid ""
"Read it at arm's length without a magnifier, then with your phone camera's "
"zoom. If you have to guess a digit, it fails."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/label"
msgid ""
"Score the **grooves**, not whether you can still guess the number. A "
"blurred 215 that you recognise because the blocks above and below are 220 "
"and 210 is still a fail."
msgstr ""

#. feature name
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/smooth"
msgid "Smooth face (surface quality)"
msgstr ""

#. feature short
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/smooth"
msgid "Surface"
msgstr ""

#. feature what
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/smooth"
msgid ""
"**Front-right face:** the whole face is plain. Look at the layer lines and "
"the sheen. Only confirms."
msgstr ""

#. feature pass
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/smooth"
msgid "Even, uniform layer lines with a consistent sheen."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail_hot
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/smooth"
msgid "Wavy or uneven lines, glossy smears, zits or ooze marks."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail_cold
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/smooth"
msgid "Matte or rough texture, an under-extruded look, gaps between layers."
msgstr ""

#. photo alt
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/smooth"
msgid ""
"The engraved label and the smooth face of two blocks: streaky and feathered "
"at 210 °C, even at 240 °C"
msgstr ""

#. photo caption
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/smooth"
msgid ""
"Top, 210 °C, too cold: the smooth face (right of the numbers) is streaky "
"and feathered, with small lumps. Bottom, 240 °C, the chosen block: even "
"layer lines."
msgstr ""

#. More: intro
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/smooth"
msgid ""
"Fails both ways: hot plastic smears and ripples, cold plastic looks dry and "
"rough. On the author's coarse PETG tower (270 → 200 °C) it showed the cold "
"end clearly: streaky, feathered layers from 220 °C down (a trace at 230), "
"even from 240 °C up; the hot blocks looked no different."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/smooth"
msgid ""
"Tilt the tower under a lamp so the light skims the face: ripples and matte "
"bands show up at once."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/smooth"
msgid ""
"Silk, matte and glitter filaments change their sheen on purpose. With them, "
"judge only the ripples and gaps, not the gloss."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/smooth"
msgid ""
"Wavy lines that look the same on **every** block are mechanical (ringing, "
"wobble), not temperature."
msgstr ""

#. feature name
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/hole"
msgid "See-through hole (roof shape)"
msgstr ""

#. feature short
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/hole"
msgid "Hole"
msgstr ""

#. feature what
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/hole"
msgid ""
"**Front rib,** next to the numbers: a round 3.5 mm hole in every block. "
"Look through it against the light: too hot, it turns into a **teardrop**. "
"**Not counted.**"
msgstr ""

#. feature pass
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/hole"
msgid ""
"A round opening with a clean, evenly curved top; nothing hangs inside."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail_hot
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/hole"
msgid ""
"The top droops or comes to a point (a teardrop); strands or sagging loops "
"hang inside the hole."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail_cold
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/hole"
msgid ""
"The top is rough or gappy: separate lines, small holes in the roof, a "
"ragged outline."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/hole"
msgid ""
"Turn the **smooth face** towards you, light **behind** the tower, eye "
"**level** with the holes."
msgstr ""

#. More: intro
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/hole"
msgid ""
"The front rib is the rib at the corner between the front face (the numbers) "
"and the front-left face. A round hole has to be closed over at the top, "
"layer by layer: when the plastic is too hot, that roof sags. The hole only "
"confirms what the other columns say. Mostly shows heat."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/hole"
msgid ""
"Turn the tower so the **front-right (smooth) face** faces you, square. The "
"front rib now stands out on your **left**, face-on, with one hole per block."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/hole"
msgid ""
"Put a lamp or a bright window **behind** the tower and look through the "
"holes **level with them**: the hole shows as a bright shape on the dark "
"rib. Compare its top with the blocks above and below."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/hole"
msgid ""
"Round is good. A top that sags into a point, like a drop of water upside "
"down, means that block was too hot."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/hole"
msgid ""
"The hole's last layers close over the gap as a short bridge: PrusaSlicer "
"prints them at the bridge speed with the bridge fan, the same on every "
"block. No other bridge or steep overhang prints in those layers (the long "
"beam starts 1 mm above the hole), so the roof's shape depends on the "
"block's temperature, not on another feature."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/hole"
msgid ""
"Looking from above or below. From an angle, the inside of the hole and the "
"beam behind it cut into the top of the light, and every hole looks "
"flattened. Keep your eye level with the hole you are reading."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/hole"
msgid ""
"Every hole the same, round or slightly pointed. That is normal for many "
"filaments and says nothing about temperature; it is why this column is not "
"counted. Only a change from block to block is worth a mark."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/hole"
msgid ""
"A tiny bump at the very top that looks the same on every block is where the "
"last line closed the roof, not a droop. Judge the overall shape, block "
"against block."
msgstr ""

#. feature name
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/flag"
msgid "Snap flag (layer adhesion)"
msgstr ""

#. feature short
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/flag"
msgid "Flag"
msgstr ""

#. feature what
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/flag"
msgid ""
"**Back-left face, on the shelf:** a thin flag at the shelf's outer edge. "
"Break it with a fingernail: how it breaks tells how well the layers fused. "
"**Colder is never better here, and it decides first.**"
msgstr ""

#. feature pass
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/flag"
msgid "It bends first, resists, and breaks with a jagged or whitened edge."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail_hot
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/flag"
msgid "The flag is already wavy or blobby before you test it."
msgstr ""

#. feature fail_cold
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/flag"
msgid ""
"It snaps cleanly and flat along one layer line, with very little force."
msgstr ""

#. point
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/flag"
msgid ""
"Break the flags **last**, with the tower fully cooled: fingernail on the "
"**top edge**, push **towards the tower**."
msgstr ""

#. photo alt
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/flag"
msgid ""
"The broken flag stumps of two blocks: a smooth, flat break on a cold block, "
"a jagged, whitened break at 240 °C"
msgstr ""

#. photo caption
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/flag"
msgid ""
"Top, too cold: a clean, flat break along one layer. Bottom, 240 °C, the "
"chosen block: a jagged, whitened break."
msgstr ""

#. stand-in photo alt
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/flag"
msgid ""
"The author's PETG temperature tower after the test, string ladder on the "
"left; three broken flags lie on the cloth in front of it"
msgstr ""

#. stand-in photo caption
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/flag"
msgid ""
"The author's tower after breaking the three coldest flags (200, 210 and 220 "
"°C): the pieces lie on the cloth. A close-up comparing the breaks is still "
"to come."
msgstr ""

#. More: intro
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/flag"
msgid ""
"The flag is one line thick, about 3 mm from the wall, with nothing above "
"its top edge. Shows cold."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/flag"
msgid ""
"Break the flags **last**, after you have scored everything else on the "
"block, and with the tower fully cooled."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/flag"
msgid ""
"Put a fingernail on the flag's **top edge** and push it **towards the "
"tower** until it breaks. Every block's flag can be reached this way; you do "
"not need to pull."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (beginner)
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/flag"
msgid ""
"Go from the top (coldest) block down. The first block where breaking feels "
"clearly harder and the edge looks jagged or whitened is your coldest "
"acceptable block."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (expert)
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/flag"
msgid ""
"Compare the force between blocks rather than judging one flag alone; a "
"single line is weaker than any real part. For parts that must be strong, "
"choose at least one block above the coldest one that passes."
msgstr ""

#. More: point
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/flag"
msgid ""
"**TPU:** the flag is not scored. TPU bends instead of snapping: mark it – "
"on every block."
msgstr ""

#. More: point (misread)
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/flag"
msgid ""
"Wet filament also makes layers weak and brittle at every temperature. If no "
"flag passes, dry the spool and print again."
msgstr ""

#. More: photo alt
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/flag"
msgid ""
"One hand holds the PETG tower from the top; a fingertip of the other hand "
"pushes a flag on the back-left face towards the tower; a broken flag lies "
"on the cloth"
msgstr ""

#. More: photo caption
msgctxt "features/temperature-tower/flag"
msgid ""
"Hold the tower from the top with one hand, put a fingertip on the flag's "
"top edge and push towards the tower. A flag broken a moment earlier lies on "
"the cloth."
msgstr ""
